WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.783 --> 00:00:08.430
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.303
Thank you very much for joining us today.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.580
So we have the pleasure of having

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.660
both Professor Theresa Betancourt,

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.770
who is the Salem Professor

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.230
in Global Practice at the Boston College

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.195
School of Social Work.

00:00:24.195 --> 00:00:27.140
She's also the Director
of the Research Program

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:31.760
on Children and Adversity.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.040
And we have one of her
collaborators, Bhuwan Gautam,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.610
who is the Senior Community Advisor

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.120
of the Bhutanese Society
of Western Massachusetts,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.120
and an FSI-R Community
Advisor at the RPCA.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.877
They're gonna be talking today about

00:00:52.877 --> 00:00:55.410
Family-based mental health promotion

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.240
for Somali Bantu and Bhutanese refugees:

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.013
The results of feasibility
and acceptability trial.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.630
- Thank you so much, Theresa,

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:15.240
and thank you, everyone,
in the program at Yale

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.160
for inviting us today.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.970
It's really a honor on
this very momentous day

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:24.720
in the history of the United
States to be giving this talk,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.180
especially to focus on New Americans

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.180
with a refugee life experience

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.860
and our work in community-based
participatory research

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.100
to develop an intervention
and to share with you

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:38.160
some of our early findings
on feasibility, acceptability

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.190
and where we'd hope to go next.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.410
So I'm Theresa Betancourt.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:43.860
And Bhuwan.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.310
- I am Bhuwan Gautam,

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.530
and thank you for the
introduction earlier.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.180
And I'll also let you know that

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.190
I am sharing a lot of my
lived refugee experiences

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.410
in addition to our
current research program

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.650
with children and adversity (indistinct)

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:00.483
- Thank you, Bhuwan,

00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:01.607
it's such an honor to have you here

00:02:01.607 --> 00:02:04.610
and to share this platform with you.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:08.680
So I'd like to just give
a little more information

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:12.715
about where I'd like to
go with the presentation,

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:15.550
some background on

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.900
our Research Program on
Children and Adversity

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.440
and its conceptual drivers.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.830
And then Bhuwan is going to talk about

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.751
the principles of community-based
participatory research,

00:02:23.751 --> 00:02:26.840
and its use within two communities,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.751
the Somali Bantu refugee community,

00:02:29.751 --> 00:02:32.390
starting first in Chelsea, Massachusetts,

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.550
and then moving out to Louis and Maine.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:35.940
I'll cover some of that,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.570
and then particularly
from his lived experience,

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:40.470
talking about the Bhutanese
refugee experience.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:41.960
And then we'll talk about

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.210
the intervention that we co-created

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:48.170
and our early findings on
feasibility and acceptability,

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.910
and a recently published trial

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:51.490
in the Journal of Adolescent Health.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.580
And then we'll talk a bit about COVID-19

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.230
and how we are iterating and adapting

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:58.754
and using technology

00:02:58.754 --> 00:03:02.380
as the team adjust to this global pandemic

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.079
and how to still serve families

00:03:04.079 --> 00:03:07.297
in the community-based
participatory research

00:03:07.297 --> 00:03:09.460
and intervention study.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.150
So as you heard in the introduction,

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.200
I direct a Research Program
on Children and Adversity.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.440
And in this work,

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.990
our focus is to really
understand factors and processes

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.720
that contribute to risky life outcomes,

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.219
as well as resilient life outcomes

00:03:24.219 --> 00:03:26.620
amongst children, youth, families,

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.492
and their broader communities
that face adversity globally.

00:03:30.492 --> 00:03:33.640
And the focus in this work
is not just on deficits,

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.520
but on strengths and capacities.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.358
And in learning about
this sort of information,

00:03:38.358 --> 00:03:42.070
we hope to contribute to
developing an evidence-based

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:44.370
that's based on local
strengths and resources,

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.130
and also helps us close this know-do gap,

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.131
that we know a lot about the
science of early adversity

00:03:50.131 --> 00:03:53.480
and its impact on child
development and mental health.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.210
But we still see very large treatment gaps

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:58.270
in low resource settings,

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.350
both the United States
and around the world.

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:02.234
And we need to be doing
our highest quality,

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:04.250
most efficient and effective work

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:05.600
in those low resource settings

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.890
to address racial and ethnic
disparities in health.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:13.010
So we have a range of research
projects within our lab.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.183
They sort of fall into
these three broad domains.

00:04:16.183 --> 00:04:18.746
Parts of global work on
war-affected children,

00:04:18.746 --> 00:04:21.080
we've had research projects underway

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.533
on the Ethiopia-Eritrea border,

00:04:22.533 --> 00:04:25.690
in the Russian Federation
with the Chechen displacement

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:28.233
and child soldiers in Northern Uganda

00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:30.560
and internally displaced persons.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.570
And then for now 18 years,

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.306
we've been working in
Sierra Leone, West Africa,

00:04:35.306 --> 00:04:38.220
building from an
intergenerational study of war

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.620
following a cohort of 529 young people,

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.640
including many former child soldiers

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.370
as they become young adults,

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.000
right now with four
waves of data collection,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.040
and this has been funded by the

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.280
National Institute of Child
Health and Human Development.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.810
And we now have 15 year follow-up

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.640
of this cohort just published in J-CAP,

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.700
which we're very honored
to have that opportunity.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:02.830
And then we've been working

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.871
on other forms of adversity in Rwanda.

00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:08.870
We started first on the topic
of HIV/AIDS affected families

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.327
and in partnership with the government,

00:05:10.327 --> 00:05:14.460
as well as the World
Bank and others, USAID,

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.120
have moved towards a focus,
not just specific to HIV,

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.847
but on children in extreme poverty,

00:05:19.847 --> 00:05:23.890
and using a family-based
preventative intervention

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:27.230
that's been adapted to focus
on early childhood development

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:28.670
and violence prevention.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:32.510
And so we've just published the
pre-post results of a trial,

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:33.680
a cluster-randomized trial

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.800
of over a thousand families in Rwanda

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.750
with the intervention
we call Sugira Muryango.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:39.790
And that actually

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.760
is the same family
strengthening intervention

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.790
that is at the base of the intervention

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.680
that Bhuwan and I will talk about today

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.680
in our project on promoting resilience

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.410
and healthy parent-child relationships

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:54.040
in New American families
in the United States.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.610
And this work is sponsored by the

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.100
National Institute on Minority
Health and Health Disparities

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.570
And we've had an R24 as well as an R01.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.080
And we're going to share
with you the early results

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:06.220
from the R24 and the R01 is underway.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.140
So Bhuwan, I'll turn it over to you.

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.203
- Sure, thank you, Dr. Betancourt.

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:12.773
There is a background
image, as you can see.

00:06:12.773 --> 00:06:13.930
A lots of the times

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:18.930
when I talk in this kind
of forums, people ask me,

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.610
what does the refugee camp hut look like,

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.650
the house look like, and
this is what it looks like.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.420
In 2015, I went to a refugee camp in Nepal

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:31.830
where I became refugee
for 15 and a half years

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:33.930
before I came to this country.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.870
And I took this picture,

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:39.050
which is one of my friend's
house and which is really small.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:40.410
And that's really ties

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:44.170
into this largest humanitarian disaster

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:48.250
that's happening since World War II.

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:51.820
And again, that's going
in the middle of pandemic.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.210
Look at this numbers.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.040
Globally at the end of 2019,

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.263
there were about 80 million
people forcibly displaced.

00:06:58.263 --> 00:07:00.450
And if you see

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:03.070
the number of children
living in the conflict zone,

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:07.910
which rose by 74% of the last decade.

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.880
So if you think the United States,

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:11.880
which we will talk about,

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.074
I will show you more data

00:07:13.074 --> 00:07:18.074
about how the resettlement
landscape has changed,

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.510
and that really needs
additional interventions

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:25.590
to address the needs of
the refugee population

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.730
who complete the unique needs.

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:30.600
- Yeah, thank you, Bhuwan.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.970
When we talk about the unique needs

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:35.180
of refugee children and their development,

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.013
their mental health,

00:07:36.013 --> 00:07:38.770
we have to go all the way
back to Urie Bronfenbrenner

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.540
and think developmentally, ecologically,

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.270
we have to think about the
role of time and maturation.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:47.560
So certainly, on to genetic
or individual factors matter,

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.530
age, gender, temperament, intelligence,

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.430
your genetic history.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:53.570
But we also have to think about

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.510
what's going on at the level of family,

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:58.500
the nature of caregiver-child
relationships,

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.449
family functioning,
caregiver mental health,

00:08:00.449 --> 00:08:02.690
resources in the family.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:03.570
And then we can't stop there,

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:05.580
we also have to think about the nested

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.690
and interrelatedness of
the community ecosystem,

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:12.510
who else is available in
the life of that young child

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:16.410
in terms of mentors,
coaches, religious figures,

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.050
other supports, extended
family, peer group,

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.892
which becomes all the more important

00:08:20.892 --> 00:08:23.860
over the process of development.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:25.990
And then as we see in our country today,

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:30.430
all of this is very much
nested within the historical,

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:33.070
the political, the social,
and the cultural context.

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:34.890
And this also relates to

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:37.930
beliefs about reconciliation,
healing and moving forward.

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:39.780
So all of these come into play

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:43.169
when we think about
anyone child's outcomes.

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:44.440
Bhuwan.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:49.440
- So if we look at the refugee
resettlement trajectory

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.783
as compared to last year,

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:57.270
not last, before the
current administration,

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.700
we used to have 70,000 refugees each year.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:05.080
That's when the U.S. government
had the highest ceiling.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.960
But after the Trump
administration took off his,

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.500
it was drastically reduced down.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:13.550
And if you look at the fiscal year 2021,

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:17.700
which is 15,000 refugees, and
that's the maximum ceiling.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.330
When you have people in the pipeline

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:20.670
and then they end up

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:25.670
coming less than the
actual ceiling limit set.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:29.550
And today's election is going to decide

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:33.620
what direction the refugee
resettlement will take,

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:37.350
which we will hopefully
see a change in this,

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:40.327
but let's wait and see.

00:09:40.327 --> 00:09:45.327
As I was mentioning before,
a person who was very young,

00:09:47.531 --> 00:09:50.270
I became a refugee when
I was seven years old

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:52.160
and having to spend lot of time,

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.240
my childhood and then
adulthood in a refugee camp.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.830
I was exposed to different factors

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:03.350
that increase the risk of
poor mental health outcomes.

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:04.183
And I'm coming to the United States

00:10:04.183 --> 00:10:07.920
along with 100,000 other folks like me,

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:09.127
and not only Bhutanese,

00:10:09.127 --> 00:10:11.940
but there are millions of
other refugees and immigrants

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.400
coming with these different
forms of hardships

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:17.940
they have with them.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:21.240
So the data shows that
children in the U.S.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.600
have very poor access to
mental health services

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:29.194
and situation has increased in refugees

00:10:29.194 --> 00:10:31.323
from the data we have found.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:33.874
- [Theresa] Thank you, Bhuwan.

00:10:33.874 --> 00:10:38.874
- And also, when refugees
come to this country,

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.280
they have many barriers as
compared to the U.S. population.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:46.600
For example, refugee folks

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:50.650
are really reluctant to seek out services

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:54.100
because of the stigma in mental health.

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:57.600
And also because of the language barriers

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.580
and cultural differences,

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:04.021
there is lack of resources
in the areas they live,

00:11:04.021 --> 00:11:07.020
and most families are overwhelmed

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:09.393
by their own migration experiences.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:13.150
Similarly, services access is very poor,

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.110
especially for children and families

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:17.850
may not be able to
recognize their own needs.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:22.850
I mean, not to have clinical
access and mental health access

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:27.280
is what they had in their camp.

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:30.360
And when you come from a
place like refugee camp,

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.260
sometime being a refugee,

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:35.450
you don't even know what resource exist,

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.070
and sometime our parents even

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.960
may not be able to
recognize their own needs.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:43.690
And they are unaware about the services

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:47.650
as the refugee and immigrant folks are,

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:51.540
they do not have high literacy rate

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:53.850
and the resource are so low.

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:58.260
And also because of so
many other barriers,

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:00.760
there are limited to referral networks

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:03.540
from school and pediatric, et cetera,

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:06.313
to address the needs of
our refugee children.

00:12:09.073 --> 00:12:11.990
So when you see the trajectory

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:15.310
of the stages of the displacement

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:18.473
when somebody becomes refugee,

00:12:18.473 --> 00:12:22.010
and then they go through
different stages of life,

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:25.776
for example, pre-flight, if
someone had, for example,

00:12:25.776 --> 00:12:30.776
I can put myself into this
states that I was in Bhutan.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.650
As I mentioned, I was very
young, I had to leave.

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:36.240
I was the first grader actually.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:40.650
And I was exposed to the violence

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:44.260
and I had many other forms
of traumatic experiences.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:48.130
When I came to the United
States, as I mentioned in 2008,

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:53.160
again, it was a new journey for me

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.670
along with my family to go to the place

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:58.990
that they don't even know, it's
like an unknown destination.

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:00.890
Even if they said United States,

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:04.750
that means it was
completely a foreign land.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:08.060
Both of my parents are
illiterate in their own language,

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:11.090
and imagine about leaving a
country and going to a place

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:14.180
that they don't actually
know what's gonna happen.

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:16.020
And other resettlement

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:21.020
also brings migration laws
and acculturation issues.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:25.365
It's a lot when someone has to go

00:13:25.365 --> 00:13:29.520
and learn the new language and
adopt to a mainstream culture

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.030
along with the financial hardships.

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:36.450
But also to let you know that
there are protective factors

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:40.180
that refugee families bring,

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:45.180
which are really great in
terms of building together

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:50.484
as resiliency factors
from individual to family,

00:13:50.484 --> 00:13:55.484
to access, to education,
and community ties,

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:58.150
ideological and religious context.

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.420
I mean, we, refugee communities

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:03.580
are coming from a low resource settings

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:06.740
and then having to go and reintegrate

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:10.350
and then acculturate with
the mainstream society,

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:12.250
sometimes requires lot of efforts.

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:15.230
And I think this kind of
collaboration and partnership

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:18.780
has really helped us to move forward.

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:21.430
And with these positive
protective factors,

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:23.880
researchers really have
to put this together

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.920
to build the intervention
that works for refugees.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:29.299
- [Orazio] Can I please ask a question?

00:14:29.299 --> 00:14:31.274
- [Theresa] Sure.

00:14:31.274 --> 00:14:33.350
- [Orazio] Bhuwan, I had the impression

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.520
that Bhutan is a very peaceful country.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:38.020
So I am kind of completely at loss

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:41.850
that there are refugees
from Bhutan to the U.S.

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:45.510
So if you could please
share the perspective

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:48.123
like what led you to become a refugee.

00:14:49.210 --> 00:14:50.990
- I'm glad that you asked this question.

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:51.890
Thank you so much.

00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:53.420
I know that Bhutan

00:14:57.147 --> 00:14:59.720
says that it's the happiest
country on the world,

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:01.450
which you might've heard,

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:04.930
that's on these gross
national happiness, right?

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:07.100
But that's the other side of the story.

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:09.180
Now, the truth of the matter is that

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.180
Bhutan has forcibly evicted

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:14.744
over 100,000 Nepali
speaking Southern Bhutanese

00:15:14.744 --> 00:15:18.480
from its country in early 1980s to '90s,

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.400
which I was gonna talk, but that's great.

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:21.833
Then I can skip later.

00:15:23.161 --> 00:15:26.550
And I am one of those
people forcibly evicted

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:27.810
and became refugees.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:29.090
And if you see,

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:33.130
that's one of the reason that we partner

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:37.080
with Boston College and
Harvard School of Public Health

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:41.720
basically to heal the
mental health problem,

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:45.410
the torture, mental health
problem that Bhutan had created

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:48.170
so that our families could be helped.

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:52.380
We have lots of people
who were the victims

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:55.940
deported by the Bhutanese
authority, Bhutanese police.

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:59.630
We still have families separated in Bhutan

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:02.740
who are not able to join our
families, they are in jail.

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:04.600
There is a horrible
other part of the story,

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.670
which I would encourage
you to look into it,

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:08.770
and you'll have a deeper understanding

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:12.730
about the plight of
the Bhutanese refugees.

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:13.653
- [Moderator] Thank you.

00:16:15.797 --> 00:16:17.880
- [Theresa] Sorry, go ahead.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:20.480
- I have a follow-up question about these.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:24.390
So I was reading a little bit
about the situation in Bhutan

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:27.110
and the kind of policies that
they implement in schools,

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:29.840
it seems that they socialize children

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:31.123
since a very early age,

00:16:31.123 --> 00:16:33.590
and the way they communicate
with the children

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:36.293
and they convey the
importance of being happy.

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:38.780
I was wondering,

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.990
how does that affect your
measurement of well-being,

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.200
your measurement of
depression, for instance,

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:45.927
which is so susceptible

00:16:45.927 --> 00:16:49.193
to how you think about
your emotional states.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:51.073
Do you think this

00:16:51.073 --> 00:16:53.470
poses an additional challenge
for measuring depression?

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:57.940
Do you think that biomarkers
might be particularly necessary

00:16:57.940 --> 00:16:59.470
in the case of Bhutanese refugees?

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:01.420
How do you think about that?

00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:06.060
- Well, to tell you the truth,
that when I became refugee,

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:07.960
as I mentioned, that I was very young.

00:17:09.424 --> 00:17:13.520
I really started to understand
the complexity of this issue

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:16.207
when I became adult in
a refugee camp in Nepal,

00:17:16.207 --> 00:17:19.900
and I further understood
about my own rights

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:24.900
about not being able to
practice my fundamental rights

00:17:26.054 --> 00:17:28.743
is something that I'm experiencing.

00:17:30.085 --> 00:17:31.910
Whenever I go to the platform,

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:33.830
I talk about these issues,

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:35.760
when someone comes up

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:38.030
who have never heard about
the other side of the story,

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:41.740
but to go with the flow
of these presenters

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:43.190
and we have a lot to cover.

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:45.494
And Dr. Betancourt and I,

00:17:45.494 --> 00:17:46.800
initially we were talking

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:49.120
that we might have to answer
some of the questions.

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:50.960
When you have something
coming up in your mind,

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:53.380
please continue to jot down your questions

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:55.770
and we will have the
question and answer session

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:59.457
to go through them to cover
the whole presentation.

00:17:59.457 --> 00:18:02.060
- Yeah, thanks, Bhuwan.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:05.310
And I think the question by (indistinct)

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:06.227
is an important one,

00:18:06.227 --> 00:18:08.988
a little bit about some of
the formative work that we did

00:18:08.988 --> 00:18:12.940
to make sure that our
measures were fit to context

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:17.940
and reflected sort of
culturally appropriate language

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:20.860
that is used by locals,

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:22.950
who may not have had the opportunity

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:26.770
to receive much education
and may not be literate,

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:28.737
but really doing a lot of formative work

00:18:28.737 --> 00:18:32.572
in a community-based
participatory research framing

00:18:32.572 --> 00:18:36.050
to learn those terms and make sure

00:18:36.050 --> 00:18:38.080
that we weren't using stigmatizing terms.

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:41.610
And then the collection of
biological data biomarkers

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:45.140
as an objective indicator
is also very interesting.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:46.370
Something we can talk about,

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:48.150
I think in the discussion a bit more,

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:49.910
because the ethical issue is also involved

00:18:49.910 --> 00:18:53.149
in that trust and relationship
and communities, as you know.

00:18:53.149 --> 00:18:57.360
So let me talk a little bit now about

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:00.899
just the co-creation process
of intervention development

00:19:00.899 --> 00:19:03.860
in collaboration with refugee communities

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:07.233
using community-based
participatory research.

00:19:08.347 --> 00:19:09.230
Bhuwan, do you wanna talk about

00:19:09.230 --> 00:19:11.020
some of our different partners?

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:14.230
- I wanna talk a little bit
of the history of the project,

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:15.940
and accolades, different partners

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:18.520
involved with Dr. Betancourt and I

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:22.180
are having the opportunity
to speak with you right now,

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:23.760
but as you can see in the list,

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:25.450
we have several others.

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:29.730
For example, we have Maine
Immigrant Refugee Services

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:36.180
and Jewish Family Services
of Western Massachusetts.

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:38.680
And oddly, partnership that began

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:41.590
even before I joined Dr. Betancourt

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:43.390
with Chelsea Collaboratives and RIAC

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.560
and other academic collaborators,

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:50.980
both at Harvard University

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:55.360
and University of Michigan and
Boston Children's Hospital.

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:57.190
And our funding support from

00:19:57.190 --> 00:19:59.930
National Institute of Minority
Health and Health Disparity

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:03.520
and Community Consultants.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:08.520
We have fantastic collaborators
that are really helping us

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:12.583
to develop this whole research
and intervention project.

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:19.390
So that leads to real nice definition

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:21.623
from Kellogg Foundation.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:26.400
Community-based
participatory research, CBPR

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:28.740
is a collaborative approach

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:31.590
to research that equitably involves

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:33.440
all the partners in the research process

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:38.440
and recognizes the unique
experience that it brings.

00:20:38.599 --> 00:20:42.420
CBPR begins with a research topic

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:44.072
of importance to the community,

00:20:44.072 --> 00:20:46.960
has the aim of combining
knowledge with action

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:50.100
and achieving social change
to improve health outcome

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:53.000
and eliminate health disparities.

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:58.000
So again, the CBPR engages researchers

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:02.130
and community members like me

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:05.609
to equitable partnership

00:21:05.609 --> 00:21:08.350
to deconstruct the power differential

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:14.447
and also community members are
engaged in all the processes.

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:18.923
Let me give you some of
the features of the CBPR,

00:21:18.923 --> 00:21:21.433
because this is a
process of power sharing.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:26.850
We come up with the challenges,
and to address that,

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:31.539
we are focused on the strengths
to offset the weaknesses,

00:21:31.539 --> 00:21:32.930
and community members

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:35.930
are engaged in each step
of the research process.

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:39.370
So CBPR is especially helpful

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:42.423
when working with a stigmatized community

00:21:42.423 --> 00:21:45.509
or with a stigmatized topic,

00:21:45.509 --> 00:21:50.280
because it allows you
to gain deeper insights

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:55.280
and lead enhanced understanding
that creates deeper buy-in

00:21:55.680 --> 00:22:00.480
and motivation necessary
to encounter stigma.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:04.947
So the advantages are to
increase the knowledge

00:22:04.947 --> 00:22:09.947
and to build bridges and
enhances the research process.

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:15.230
So it might take a lot time and resource

00:22:17.170 --> 00:22:19.490
to develop the partnership.

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:20.850
But at the end of the day,

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:24.320
we are thinking of building
the social capital strength

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:29.523
in those low resource setting communities.

00:22:33.253 --> 00:22:37.980
As I can emphasis that there
is a limited implication

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:39.170
so far in mental health,

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.170
and we are so glad

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:44.810
that we are sharing our work with you all.

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:47.634
CBPR has promising approach

00:22:47.634 --> 00:22:51.150
given the stigma around mental health.

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:53.900
And it's really important to
understand the local context

00:22:53.900 --> 00:22:56.560
and language around the
mental health problem

00:22:56.560 --> 00:22:59.830
that can improve the community engagement

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:02.883
and inform the intervention.

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:11.430
So our CBPR approach is
by refugees for refugees,

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:13.487
we have unique approach.

00:23:13.487 --> 00:23:17.880
We have two community
board advisory boards,

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:21.250
one is Youth CAB, we call Youth CAB.

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:22.900
And in Youth CAB

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:25.133
we have middle schoolers
and high schoolers.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:28.650
Because the research program

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:30.973
is for the children and the parents,

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:34.363
we continue to seek advice

00:23:34.363 --> 00:23:38.330
from both children's point of view also,

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:42.160
and from the parents
who are the CAB members,

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:45.543
adult CAB members sharing
their feedback, the results,

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:52.260
which will help us
document their feedback.

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:57.000
And we will develop
culturally and linguistically

00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:00.303
appropriate intervention
in our FSI module.

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:05.550
So community outreach events we have,

00:24:05.550 --> 00:24:10.550
we try to spread the work that
we do as much as possible,

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:13.838
not leaving anyone in the community.

00:24:13.838 --> 00:24:18.170
And also we have monthly meetings,

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:21.720
and I am one of the liaison with

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:26.360
Boston School of Social
Work with Dr. Betancourt.

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:29.030
And our job, and not only me,

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:31.159
I have other colleagues who are not here,

00:24:31.159 --> 00:24:36.159
our job is to advise on our
social and cultural needs.

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:41.833
- Great.

00:24:41.833 --> 00:24:44.340
So I'll give a little background

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:46.320
on the Somali Bantu refugee community,

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:48.390
and then turn it over for Bhuwan

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:49.650
to give a little more of a history

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:52.370
of the Bhutanese displacement.

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:54.336
So the Somali Bantu refugee community

00:24:54.336 --> 00:24:58.700
is actually a subset of the
overall Somali displacement

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:01.990
and resettlement around the world.

00:25:01.990 --> 00:25:05.050
The Somali Bantu peoples were
originally generations ago,

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:07.330
taken from other parts of Africa

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:10.700
and served as slaves in Somalia.

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:13.700
When the war broke out in 1991,

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:17.150
they were displaced along with
the broader Somali majority

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:20.260
to refugee camps originally,
and then also resettled.

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:21.399
Although the early displacement

00:25:21.399 --> 00:25:23.920
were from Somali majority families

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:26.560
who had access to
education and connections,

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:29.550
but there was a decision
of the Obama administration

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:32.680
to take a large number
of Somali Bantu as well.

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:34.300
But their trauma history is quite high.

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:37.460
As you might imagine, very
little access to school,

00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:41.720
historically, prolonged trauma exposure,

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:43.750
both just within their families,

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:45.910
as well as in the refugee camps,

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:48.228
Dadaab and Kakuma refugee camps in Kenya,

00:25:48.228 --> 00:25:52.090
and have had very little
access to education.

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:53.380
So a lot of the women,

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:57.020
the mothers we work with, may
not have literacy in Mai-Mai,

00:25:57.020 --> 00:25:58.643
it's mainly a spoken language.

00:25:58.643 --> 00:26:00.970
And so English as a second language

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:04.640
or English language learning
has been very complicated.

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:05.830
And we'll see situations

00:26:05.830 --> 00:26:07.410
where children are learning English,

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:08.886
but especially the mothers

00:26:08.886 --> 00:26:12.640
haven't had the basics to
allow them to have a platform

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.570
to succeed in those sorts
of training programs.

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:17.380
We need a different type
of literacy education

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:19.780
and integrated approaches.

00:26:19.780 --> 00:26:22.060
Overall, Somalis are the largest single

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:24.900
group of resettled African
refugees in U.S. history.

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:27.210
And then the Somali Bantu
are a subset within this.

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:31.140
In 2004, over 13,000 Somali Bantu

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:33.110
were resettled in 50 communities

00:26:33.110 --> 00:26:36.080
across 38 states, United States.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:37.130
So in Boston,

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:40.120
resettlement began the Boston
area in 2004 with two families

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:42.400
and soon expanded to over 400.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:44.979
And then there was secondary migration

00:26:44.979 --> 00:26:47.700
to states where jobs were more plentiful,

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:49.620
where housing was more affordable,

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:51.656
including to Maine, and Louis and Maine

00:26:51.656 --> 00:26:54.627
being a very large population
of both Somali majority

00:26:54.627 --> 00:26:56.730
and Somali Bantu refugees.

00:26:56.730 --> 00:26:58.330
And there've been historical tensions,

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:00.760
but also a lot of shared culture,

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:02.970
history experience between
the two communities.

00:27:02.970 --> 00:27:06.880
And many of our partners
are coming from both sides.

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:08.957
This project's mainly been
done with the Somali Bantu

00:27:08.957 --> 00:27:11.640
because of the language and
the cultural differences,

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:13.120
but some of our greatest collaborators

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:15.877
are also majority who are
trusted by the community

00:27:15.877 --> 00:27:18.260
and work very closely with them.

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:20.630
Over to you, Bhuwan.

00:27:20.630 --> 00:27:24.780
- So I'm gonna provide
a background of Bhutan.

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:27.532
Bhutan is a tiny Himalayan country

00:27:27.532 --> 00:27:30.160
sandwiched between India and China,

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:31.900
which is really isolated kingdom

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:35.584
of about 700,000 population.

00:27:35.584 --> 00:27:38.983
And as I was speaking before,

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:44.460
Bhutan government evicted its citizen

00:27:44.460 --> 00:27:48.340
by adopting ethnic cleansing
policy in early '90s,

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:53.360
evicting 100,000 ethnic Nepalese,

00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:58.360
we call Lhotsampa, that we
speak Nepali language in Bhutan,

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:00.920
under One Nation One People policy,

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:03.360
which is also known as Bhutanization,

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:06.520
that targeted cultural and
religious and traditional

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:08.650
and majority were Hindus,

00:28:08.650 --> 00:28:11.940
but also we have Buddhist
and Christians as well,

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:13.713
who were evicted.

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:19.670
And also our parents' citizenship
rights were eliminated

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:24.960
and we all were forced to
leave to neighboring countries.

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:27.840
And we had to cross
India to get to Bhutan.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:31.330
And I remember following
the footsteps of my parents,

00:28:31.330 --> 00:28:35.160
I was young, but then I
remember slipping on the woods.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:39.903
It took so many days to get
there in refugee camp Nepal.

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:43.550
And in a refugee camp, we were settled.

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:45.590
This is one of the pictures
that I was talking about,

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:48.280
in this kind of setting
for 15 and a half years,

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:50.513
there was no bathroom, not toilet inside.

00:28:51.490 --> 00:28:53.150
There's a lot of public health problem,

00:28:53.150 --> 00:28:56.820
which I am able to recognize that.

00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:00.225
But again, we got survived and I'm here.

00:29:00.225 --> 00:29:05.225
That's why it's really important
to deal with the hardships

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:08.617
and problem that refugees bring

00:29:08.617 --> 00:29:11.260
to address in a culturally

00:29:11.260 --> 00:29:14.110
and linguistically and appropriate way.

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:16.920
And many escape the violence.

00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:19.001
And at the time I became refugee.

00:29:19.001 --> 00:29:21.660
When I was in high school,

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:24.153
there was a civil war in Nepal

00:29:24.153 --> 00:29:28.770
that had affected many of
children in Nepal as well.

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:32.690
And there was really
difficulty, not even difficult,

00:29:32.690 --> 00:29:34.550
I mean, more than difficult,

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:36.000
refugees are not allowed

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:40.210
to go outside their
refugee camps to school.

00:29:40.210 --> 00:29:42.160
The maximum you could go,

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:45.740
I could go in my refugee
camp was 10th grade,

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:47.962
and you're done, no education after that.

00:29:47.962 --> 00:29:50.540
Employment, forget about
it, there is no employment.

00:29:50.540 --> 00:29:53.997
You have to survive your
life on the basic food,

00:29:53.997 --> 00:29:56.180
(indistinct) provided by UNHCR.

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:59.943
And there was a discrimination
from the local Nepalese.

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:03.140
- [Orazio] Bhuwan, I have a question.

00:30:03.140 --> 00:30:04.290
- [Bhuwan] Yes.

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:05.860
- [Orazio] What was the relationship

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:09.190
like before Bhutanese were driven out?

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:17.500
Was the relationship very
complimentary or like was...

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:20.110
I'm still in shock,

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:23.021
I had not expected that
at all from Bhutan.

00:30:23.021 --> 00:30:23.854
- Sure.

00:30:23.854 --> 00:30:28.854
I mean, Bhutan, it is a
monarchial system of government,

00:30:29.190 --> 00:30:31.390
there is a king and prime minister.

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:35.130
And Bhutan had no problem before 1980s.

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:38.670
But when the Bhutanese people,

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:41.050
Nepali speaking people became educated

00:30:41.050 --> 00:30:43.973
and they started demanding
about the fundamental rights

00:30:46.083 --> 00:30:47.785
and it became a problem.

00:30:47.785 --> 00:30:50.180
the community became target,

00:30:50.180 --> 00:30:53.500
and also the other social
political influences.

00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:57.000
Nepal was going through political reforms

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:58.070
as well as in India

00:30:58.070 --> 00:31:02.961
and all kinds of neighboring
influences geopolitical issues.

00:31:02.961 --> 00:31:04.677
- [Orazio] Okay, thank you.

00:31:05.937 --> 00:31:09.140
- And Bhuwan's written some
very important commentaries

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:10.017
we can share with the group,

00:31:10.017 --> 00:31:12.596
and there've been these
events in New England around,

00:31:12.596 --> 00:31:15.000
Bhutan, the happiest kingdom on earth,

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:17.950
and not recognizing this
truth of their history,

00:31:17.950 --> 00:31:19.743
it's much more complex than that.

00:31:21.408 --> 00:31:24.279
- So there is increased
mental health problems

00:31:24.279 --> 00:31:27.920
when refugees, they come to this country

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:29.730
due to the resettlement stressors,

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:32.130
including cultural adaptation, language

00:31:32.130 --> 00:31:35.170
and overwhelming information, anxiety,

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:39.310
which is likely to remain
for prolonged period of time.

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:40.820
And the fear for future,

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:44.240
resettlement in crime affected areas,

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:48.810
and also refugees need to
compete in the job market

00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:52.220
all of a sudden after 20
years of jobless life,

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:54.160
which is not easy.

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:58.950
And as you see in a Bhutanese
refugees context in the U.S.,

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:01.800
third country resettlement began in 2007.

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:04.090
And I was the first person

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:06.150
to come in Springville, Massachusetts.

00:32:06.150 --> 00:32:10.560
Before that I had no idea
about what Massachusetts was.

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:13.300
In fact, I was told you
are going to Windsor Locks.

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:14.280
What is Windsor Locks?

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:17.910
I didn't even have internet
to look up Windsor Locks.

00:32:17.910 --> 00:32:18.743
And in fact,

00:32:18.743 --> 00:32:22.120
I found that it was the
place where the airport,

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:24.003
Bradley International Airport was.

00:32:25.968 --> 00:32:30.180
People think that when refugees
are brought to America,

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:31.280
the problem is solved.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:34.840
No, there is a lot of complexities.

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:38.940
And after Bhutanese
refugees came to the U.S.,

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:42.300
CDC published a report that

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:44.440
suicide rate among the Bhutanese refugees

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:48.913
was almost three times more
than the national level.

00:32:50.054 --> 00:32:52.300
- Three times, times as high, yeah.

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:55.080
Very alarming rate of suicide
around the United States.

00:32:55.080 --> 00:32:55.913
- Yes.

00:32:59.037 --> 00:33:01.560
- So thank you, Bhuwan,

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:02.630
very important history

00:33:02.630 --> 00:33:06.840
and also justifies why
mental health was of interest

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:10.373
when we invited the Bhutanese
to join our partnership.

00:33:10.373 --> 00:33:14.490
And the partnership had
started back in 2004

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:17.570
first with the Somali
Bantu in the Boston area.

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:18.620
And so at that time,

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:20.240
I was working at Boston Medical Center

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:22.804
with Heidi Ellis and others
at their refugee center

00:33:22.804 --> 00:33:24.820
for trauma and resilience.

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:26.320
And we had been contacted

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:29.900
by the Lynn Public
Schools in Massachusetts,

00:33:29.900 --> 00:33:32.905
because they'd had a large
influx of Somali Bantu children

00:33:32.905 --> 00:33:35.370
into their classrooms.

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:37.945
And there were behavioral
problems arising,

00:33:37.945 --> 00:33:40.155
especially in the younger grades.

00:33:40.155 --> 00:33:43.683
When teachers would ask
things like time to line up.

00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:47.830
And Somali Bantu kids

00:33:47.830 --> 00:33:50.493
come pushing and shoving
to the front of the line,

00:33:50.493 --> 00:33:53.870
and the teachers felt that
this was bad behavior,

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:56.370
but these were kids who
back in the refugee camps,

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:58.400
those had been really
important survival behaviors,

00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:00.130
if food was being distributed,

00:34:00.130 --> 00:34:02.070
you did need to push to
the front of the line.

00:34:02.070 --> 00:34:03.755
And so there was just a misunderstanding

00:34:03.755 --> 00:34:05.250
between the two contexts,

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:06.450
the teachers weren't understanding

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:07.850
the children and the families,

00:34:07.850 --> 00:34:09.960
and the parents themselves
didn't really understand

00:34:09.960 --> 00:34:11.200
how to navigate the school.

00:34:11.200 --> 00:34:12.980
So in Somalia,

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:15.300
it's respectful to turn your child

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:18.930
over to the mentorship of the teacher

00:34:18.930 --> 00:34:21.120
and to not get in the teacher's hair.

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:24.100
So they weren't coming to
parent-teacher meetings

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:24.990
or coming to the school

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:27.450
and getting to know the school community.

00:34:27.450 --> 00:34:29.830
And the school saw that
as them being disengaged,

00:34:29.830 --> 00:34:31.860
but really they were
trying to be respectful.

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:33.920
So there was a big disconnect happening.

00:34:33.920 --> 00:34:36.980
And we were asked to come in
and do some awareness raising,

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:39.060
which we did with the
administration and the teachers,

00:34:39.060 --> 00:34:40.180
and also the community.

00:34:40.180 --> 00:34:41.013
And at that time,

00:34:41.013 --> 00:34:44.510
I met Aweis Hussein, who was
serving as a school liaison.

00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:45.900
He himself was Somali Bantu,

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:49.060
he's now gone on to
finish his nursing degree,

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:50.530
and he's an amazing person.

00:34:50.530 --> 00:34:51.950
And he helped us kick off this project.

00:34:51.950 --> 00:34:54.272
We wrote some early
planning grants together,

00:34:54.272 --> 00:34:56.830
and building this community
partnership, as you can see,

00:34:56.830 --> 00:35:00.630
has taken time to get to this
place of having NIH funding.

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:02.297
But it started with this collaboration

00:35:02.297 --> 00:35:06.330
to meet both community and research goals,

00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:08.580
because we were interested
in risk and resilience

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:10.010
in more affected children,

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.680
including those who come

00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:14.060
and resettle the United
States with their families.

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:15.410
So here's a picture of a Aweis,

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:17.495
you see him down on the
second row in the middle

00:35:17.495 --> 00:35:19.080
at the Chelsea Collab.

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:22.180
And the Chelsea Collaborative
is an amazing place.

00:35:22.180 --> 00:35:25.145
Chelsea is the city across
the river from Boston.

00:35:25.145 --> 00:35:28.720
It's largely migrant,
very diverse community.

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:30.880
It's been hard hit by COVID-19 actually.

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:31.970
A lot of frontline workers,

00:35:31.970 --> 00:35:34.690
a lot of people living in crowded housing

00:35:34.690 --> 00:35:36.130
with food insecurity.

00:35:36.130 --> 00:35:37.120
And this wonderful group

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:38.653
that Chelsea Collaborative was mentoring

00:35:38.653 --> 00:35:42.065
the Somali Bantu Community Association

00:35:42.065 --> 00:35:44.851
to become their own mutual
assistance organization

00:35:44.851 --> 00:35:47.490
and to learn leadership skills,

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:49.039
how to write grants, how to plan,

00:35:49.039 --> 00:35:51.020
which was really fabulous.

00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:52.850
We'd go in and we speak Spanish first,

00:35:52.850 --> 00:35:54.892
and then we'd be switching to Mai-Mai.

00:35:54.892 --> 00:35:57.226
And that's really how this project started

00:35:57.226 --> 00:36:02.090
with that leadership training
of the Shanboro Association

00:36:02.090 --> 00:36:03.730
and the partnership
with the Aweis Hussein.

00:36:03.730 --> 00:36:05.810
And so the early aim of the partnership

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:07.806
was to try to understand
the community situation

00:36:07.806 --> 00:36:09.280
and its priorities,

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:10.960
especially related to these issues

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:14.026
of emotional behavioral health
and wellbeing of children,

00:36:14.026 --> 00:36:16.810
and bringing up their human capital

00:36:16.810 --> 00:36:18.570
and ensuring that they could thrive.

00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:23.020
So understanding the nature of
the problems and its causes,

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:25.240
as well as what are the
strengths we can leverage

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:26.782
before we ever move to solutions.

00:36:26.782 --> 00:36:29.030
And this meant we had
to improve communication

00:36:29.030 --> 00:36:31.220
because there's such issues around stigma.

00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:34.250
And so if we wanna talk
about mental health,

00:36:34.250 --> 00:36:35.130
how do we start?

00:36:35.130 --> 00:36:35.963
So we started

00:36:35.963 --> 00:36:38.890
using methods we had used
in Sub-Saharan Africa

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:42.820
and Northern Uganda and Rwanda
using first free listing,

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:44.540
and then moving to key
informant interviews

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:47.240
and getting local syndrome cover terms

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:48.338
that were non-stigmatizing

00:36:48.338 --> 00:36:50.700
for a range of different expressions

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:52.530
of emotional behavioral
problems in children.

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:53.687
And then using this information

00:36:53.687 --> 00:36:56.500
to assist in planning intervention models,

00:36:56.500 --> 00:36:58.730
because we could talk about strengths.

00:36:58.730 --> 00:37:00.399
Bhuwan, do you wanna talk
about our partnerships?

00:37:00.399 --> 00:37:01.232
- Sure.

00:37:01.232 --> 00:37:04.640
As I mentioned before that
we have multi-sectoral

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:08.030
and multi-disciplinary team
members involved in this project

00:37:08.030 --> 00:37:10.260
that includes academic partners,

00:37:10.260 --> 00:37:12.947
refugee community, resettlement agencies,

00:37:12.947 --> 00:37:17.947
which will guide the study investigators

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:20.200
in creating the shared vision

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:22.700
through this collaborative research.

00:37:22.700 --> 00:37:26.460
So our CBPR team and the
community stakeholders

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:29.451
really foster cross-cultural collaboration

00:37:29.451 --> 00:37:32.760
in order to drive research questions,

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:37.760
which will help us in the
delivery of the improved services.

00:37:38.152 --> 00:37:40.020
- [Theresa] Thank you.

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:43.938
- So our partnership, we have values,

00:37:43.938 --> 00:37:45.030
and our values

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:48.443
are really invested in
the refugee communities.

00:37:49.400 --> 00:37:54.400
This fits in saying that give man a fish,

00:37:55.490 --> 00:37:58.550
he will eat for a day,
teach him how to fish,

00:37:58.550 --> 00:38:00.003
he will eat for his life.

00:38:01.022 --> 00:38:04.063
By training the refugee folks,

00:38:04.970 --> 00:38:08.170
you will be able to
prepare them for employment

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:10.040
in an effort to skill-building

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:12.180
in a low resources communities,

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:16.060
like one of them is
Bhutanese refugee community.

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:19.100
And after the research project is over,

00:38:19.100 --> 00:38:23.960
the refugee communities will
have produced the experts

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:26.450
who will influence and inspire others

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:29.130
to go for public health schools

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:30.860
and also address some of the health

00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:34.940
and public health problems
that we had never known before.

00:38:34.940 --> 00:38:38.464
I mean, I came to this country in 2008

00:38:38.464 --> 00:38:42.140
and work at the resettlement agencies

00:38:42.140 --> 00:38:44.079
for five and a half years,

00:38:44.079 --> 00:38:48.760
till then, I had no idea
about public health issues.

00:38:48.760 --> 00:38:51.460
I mean, before I got
involved with the research

00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:55.290
in (indistinct) Community Health Center.

00:38:55.290 --> 00:38:57.950
It's a lot when a refugee community member

00:38:57.950 --> 00:39:01.713
is involved and trained
in this type of work.

00:39:04.090 --> 00:39:09.090
In a collectivist society
like Bhutanese community,

00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:12.740
these skills will build
social capital resources,

00:39:12.740 --> 00:39:13.980
which can be used

00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:17.210
by not just the Springville
Bhutanese communities,

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:19.053
but the Bhutanese diaspora.

00:39:22.743 --> 00:39:25.250
- So now I'm gonna talk a little bit

00:39:25.250 --> 00:39:29.391
about mixed methods and
how we approach this work.

00:39:29.391 --> 00:39:32.380
So this quote from Arthur Kleinman,

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:34.570
who's a very well-known
medical anthropologist

00:39:34.570 --> 00:39:35.610
and psychiatrist at Harvard

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:37.130
captures what we're up against

00:39:37.130 --> 00:39:38.550
when we think about culture.

00:39:38.550 --> 00:39:40.626
And there was an earlier
question about assessment,

00:39:40.626 --> 00:39:44.450
as well as then what you learn locally

00:39:44.450 --> 00:39:46.000
to think about intervention development.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:47.457
So as Kleinman states,

00:39:47.457 --> 00:39:48.550
"We know from ethnography

00:39:48.550 --> 00:39:50.410
convincingly that concepts of emotions,

00:39:50.410 --> 00:39:53.130
the self and the body and
general illness categories

00:39:53.130 --> 00:39:55.770
differ so significantly
in different cultures,

00:39:55.770 --> 00:39:57.500
that it can be said that
each culture's beliefs

00:39:57.500 --> 00:39:59.440
about normal and abnormal behavior

00:39:59.440 --> 00:40:01.547
are actually quite distinctive."

00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:05.630
So that's where we run into problems

00:40:05.630 --> 00:40:06.581
if we just assume

00:40:06.581 --> 00:40:11.581
the tools we use with Americans
or in Western populations

00:40:12.181 --> 00:40:14.330
can just be given a forward translation,

00:40:14.330 --> 00:40:16.400
and now they're going to
work with Bhutanese refugees

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:18.042
or Somali Bantu refugees.

00:40:18.042 --> 00:40:19.560
So we really

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:22.350
try to go about this approach
in a very different way,

00:40:22.350 --> 00:40:26.280
starting with the EMAC perspective locally

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:29.320
in qualitative formative work,

00:40:29.320 --> 00:40:32.700
to understand local constructs
related to mental health,

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:35.050
to parent-child
relationships and parenting,

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:38.430
to protective processes that
are relevant to better outcomes

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:40.970
for children and families
that face challenges.

00:40:40.970 --> 00:40:43.810
And then to use that data,
to select our measures,

00:40:43.810 --> 00:40:45.290
adapt standard measures,

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:47.190
or where there isn't a good alignment,

00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:48.160
create new measures

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:52.210
and test them through validity studies.

00:40:52.210 --> 00:40:53.640
And we also now,

00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:55.640
we're learning not just
about the problems,

00:40:55.640 --> 00:40:57.540
but about the protective resources,

00:40:57.540 --> 00:40:59.796
so the ingredients for
potential intervention models

00:40:59.796 --> 00:41:02.250
that can help promote better outcomes.

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:04.780
And so that's coming into this phase

00:41:04.780 --> 00:41:06.660
where we're informing
the intervention model,

00:41:06.660 --> 00:41:09.250
selecting intervention model
based on that formative work,

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:10.586
thinking about fit to context,

00:41:10.586 --> 00:41:13.540
and evaluate these interventions

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:15.410
using the most rigorous designs possible,

00:41:15.410 --> 00:41:17.490
including randomized controlled trials.

00:41:17.490 --> 00:41:19.090
And now in a lot of our work

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:20.883
in different parts of Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:41:20.883 --> 00:41:24.875
And even now the second
stage, R01 of this project,

00:41:24.875 --> 00:41:27.730
looking at implementation
science questions.

00:41:27.730 --> 00:41:31.200
So hybrid designs where we're
looking at effectiveness,

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:34.630
but also testing strategies
for quality improvement,

00:41:34.630 --> 00:41:38.033
fidelity monitoring, and scaling out.

00:41:39.260 --> 00:41:42.860
So we talked a little bit about
some of the formative work.

00:41:42.860 --> 00:41:43.693
If you're interested,

00:41:43.693 --> 00:41:46.430
there's a really tremendous
amount of detail in this paper,

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:48.100
we published back in 2015

00:41:48.100 --> 00:41:50.060
in the American Journal of Public Health.

00:41:50.060 --> 00:41:51.310
And this represented

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:53.790
our community-based participatory research

00:41:53.790 --> 00:41:54.730
in the two communities

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:57.820
and how we got at those local terms.

00:41:57.820 --> 00:42:00.650
We're talking in a non-stigmatizing
way about mental health.

00:42:00.650 --> 00:42:01.483
And based on that,

00:42:01.483 --> 00:42:05.190
we also did work on the
protective resources.

00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:08.800
And this work got some
really wonderful attention.

00:42:08.800 --> 00:42:10.490
We made the cover of the Harvard Magazine

00:42:10.490 --> 00:42:12.540
back when I was at Harvard for many years

00:42:14.170 --> 00:42:17.620
before professorship at Boston College.

00:42:17.620 --> 00:42:21.070
And also we were able to
have our refugee team come

00:42:21.070 --> 00:42:22.620
and help us disseminate the results,

00:42:22.620 --> 00:42:23.453
the early formative work

00:42:23.453 --> 00:42:24.898
and what we were learning
about youth issues

00:42:24.898 --> 00:42:26.411
at Chelsea City Hall,

00:42:26.411 --> 00:42:30.563
with policy makers and leaders
there in the community.

00:42:31.450 --> 00:42:33.560
And so now I wanna talk
about moving from that data

00:42:33.560 --> 00:42:36.340
to intervention, co-creation
and co-development,

00:42:36.340 --> 00:42:38.963
and talk about the family
strengthening intervention.

00:42:38.963 --> 00:42:43.017
Some of you may know the work
of Dr. William Beardslee,

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:45.160
his original intervention

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:47.290
was developed for the
prevention of depression

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:49.400
and the offspring of depressed caregivers.

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:50.840
After the formative work,

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:52.850
and after looking at
different intervention models

00:42:52.850 --> 00:42:56.281
that could be done by lay people
that were strengths-based,

00:42:56.281 --> 00:43:00.080
that really had a focus on families

00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:01.040
and their functioning

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:03.870
as a way of promoting
wellbeing in the children,

00:43:03.870 --> 00:43:05.750
we saw tremendous
alignment and opportunity

00:43:05.750 --> 00:43:07.300
with Beards' model.

00:43:07.300 --> 00:43:09.440
We had been working with Bear in Rwanda

00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:11.740
on adapting this for
HIV effected families.

00:43:11.740 --> 00:43:13.930
And then we had this aha moment of

00:43:13.930 --> 00:43:16.429
'Goodness, this is working
in Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:43:16.429 --> 00:43:20.070
we're working with Somali Bantu refugees

00:43:20.070 --> 00:43:21.877
back in the Boston area.

00:43:21.877 --> 00:43:25.180
Maybe this could also work
well with the diaspora

00:43:25.180 --> 00:43:30.180
resettling New Americans, the
refugees back in New England."

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:32.190
And so that was the initial idea.

00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:34.560
And we saw this as an exciting opportunity

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:36.090
to think about making it a fit

00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:37.740
to the setting and the context

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:40.495
of refugee families resettled
in the United States.

00:43:40.495 --> 00:43:42.110
So the conceptual model,

00:43:42.110 --> 00:43:43.510
and what I also wanna mention

00:43:43.510 --> 00:43:45.040
when it comes to implementation science

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:47.276
is that Bear's family talk intervention

00:43:47.276 --> 00:43:49.620
is registered in the

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:51.870
National Registry of Effective
Programs and Practices.

00:43:51.870 --> 00:43:54.503
So it has a pretty broad evidence-based.

00:43:54.503 --> 00:43:57.480
And in Rwanda, we adapted it more broadly,

00:43:57.480 --> 00:43:58.470
not just to depression,

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:00.370
but to other mental health
problems in children.

00:44:00.370 --> 00:44:02.500
And that was sort of the idea here too,

00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:05.780
because distressed refugee children,

00:44:05.780 --> 00:44:08.596
doesn't only present in the
form of depression, although,

00:44:08.596 --> 00:44:11.587
it can also show up in
conduct problems, anxiety.

00:44:11.587 --> 00:44:13.897
So we wanted to think
broadly about mental health

00:44:13.897 --> 00:44:15.520
and promoting mental health.

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:17.420
And so the conceptual model here is

00:44:17.420 --> 00:44:19.020
we're working with refugee families

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:21.060
that have this history of past trauma,

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:23.270
as well as the resettlement
and acculturative stressors

00:44:23.270 --> 00:44:25.120
that you've heard about from Bhuwan.

00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:26.210
Some of the risk factors

00:44:26.210 --> 00:44:27.560
that we learn about in our formative work

00:44:27.560 --> 00:44:29.260
were just limited access to services,

00:44:29.260 --> 00:44:32.210
not even knowing much about mental health

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:34.570
or understanding how to navigate

00:44:34.570 --> 00:44:36.140
and get help for the family,

00:44:36.140 --> 00:44:37.280
get help for children.

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:38.410
Lots of misunderstandings,

00:44:38.410 --> 00:44:40.480
as you heard about the
U.S. education system,

00:44:40.480 --> 00:44:42.050
also social services.

00:44:42.050 --> 00:44:44.810
We can tell you all sorts of
stories about misunderstanding,

00:44:44.810 --> 00:44:47.880
about child protective services or 911,

00:44:47.880 --> 00:44:50.440
and all those interactions,
and a lot of fear.

00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:52.740
And then just communication breakdowns.

00:44:52.740 --> 00:44:53.580
As I mentioned,

00:44:53.580 --> 00:44:55.470
oftentimes you'll have children

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:58.250
who are quickly learning English

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:00.980
because they're growing
up in U.S. culture,

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:03.293
and then you've got a mother and parents

00:45:03.293 --> 00:45:05.850
who are coming from a
very different background

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:07.320
and may not even have the ability

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:11.598
to speak the same language
in the same household.

00:45:11.598 --> 00:45:13.410
And so, of course, as you might imagine,

00:45:13.410 --> 00:45:16.135
we have issues of
intergenerational conflict,

00:45:16.135 --> 00:45:19.970
where there's a lot of
tension and poor communication

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:22.270
between children and their caregivers.

00:45:22.270 --> 00:45:24.440
And so the core intervention components

00:45:24.440 --> 00:45:26.290
building out on those models

00:45:26.290 --> 00:45:27.850
were first this navigation role,

00:45:27.850 --> 00:45:31.400
because this is done by
refugees for refugees,

00:45:31.400 --> 00:45:33.700
with home visitors being
from the community,

00:45:33.700 --> 00:45:36.280
they can help them navigate
both the formal systems,

00:45:36.280 --> 00:45:39.540
like understanding the educational system,

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:43.980
child protective services,
medical care, insurance,

00:45:43.980 --> 00:45:45.890
but also informal supports,

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:48.450
like getting additional
supports from friends,

00:45:48.450 --> 00:45:51.010
from neighbors, from
community associations.

00:45:51.010 --> 00:45:52.120
And as you heard

00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:53.990
in the protective
processes Bhuwan discussed,

00:45:53.990 --> 00:45:55.695
those mutual assistance organizations

00:45:55.695 --> 00:45:57.610
are so critical,

00:45:57.610 --> 00:45:58.940
and the community trust

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:01.410
and community leaders like
Bhuwan are so critical.

00:46:01.410 --> 00:46:02.850
And then a lot of work

00:46:02.850 --> 00:46:05.040
on just understanding the
U.S. education system.

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:07.410
And we've been focused really
on school and these children

00:46:07.410 --> 00:46:09.340
in this phase of the intervention.

00:46:09.340 --> 00:46:11.970
Although we do want to eventually work

00:46:11.970 --> 00:46:14.930
towards early childhood
development as a target.

00:46:14.930 --> 00:46:17.440
And a lovely part of Beard model,

00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:21.568
which has a nice fit for
trauma affected populations

00:46:21.568 --> 00:46:23.610
is the family narrative.

00:46:23.610 --> 00:46:25.130
And this is a chance for the family

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:29.540
to tell the story of their
experience as a family

00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:31.700
at their own pace with their own content.

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:32.533
And it's not meant

00:46:32.533 --> 00:46:35.470
to focus on retelling
past traumatic events,

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:38.009
but really to focus on the
strengths along the way,

00:46:38.009 --> 00:46:40.906
in the joys, as well as
the difficult events,

00:46:40.906 --> 00:46:43.750
what helped you make it through that time?

00:46:43.750 --> 00:46:45.620
And then where did you come from?

00:46:45.620 --> 00:46:47.360
What have you been through
and where are you going?

00:46:47.360 --> 00:46:50.113
It look towards the future
and a hope for the future,

00:46:50.113 --> 00:46:53.490
which is so critical to help
refugee families thrive.

00:46:53.490 --> 00:46:56.099
And then also just developing
positive parenting skills,

00:46:56.099 --> 00:46:57.960
thinking about routines,

00:46:57.960 --> 00:46:59.810
thinking about improving communication,

00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:03.270
thinking about natural consequences,
spending time together,

00:47:03.270 --> 00:47:04.970
that's productive and positive.

00:47:04.970 --> 00:47:07.280
Those are all a part
of additional supports

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:08.460
that parents are getting,

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:09.400
with the intention being

00:47:09.400 --> 00:47:11.540
to improve the parent-child relationship,

00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:12.450
but also diminish

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:13.970
risk of mental health
problems in children,

00:47:13.970 --> 00:47:16.430
which we know are elevated
in refugee children.

00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:18.570
So as you've heard, it's
a strengths-based model,

00:47:18.570 --> 00:47:21.859
it was meant to be brief,
can be done by lay workers,

00:47:21.859 --> 00:47:23.190
it's prevention.

00:47:23.190 --> 00:47:25.250
We're not doing in-depth treatment.

00:47:25.250 --> 00:47:27.210
And the idea here is to recognize

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:30.690
and build on existing family
strengths to enhance resilience

00:47:30.690 --> 00:47:32.440
and to use those protective resources

00:47:32.440 --> 00:47:34.480
as the active ingredients

00:47:34.480 --> 00:47:36.077
in promoting mental health in children.

00:47:36.077 --> 00:47:38.360
And so it follows a manualized protocol.

00:47:38.360 --> 00:47:39.370
We have weekly meetings

00:47:39.370 --> 00:47:41.610
between the family and the home visitor

00:47:41.610 --> 00:47:44.100
who's from the community,
speaks their language,

00:47:44.100 --> 00:47:45.420
knows their culture.

00:47:45.420 --> 00:47:47.650
And we have separate sessions
for children and adults.

00:47:47.650 --> 00:47:48.540
The early sessions

00:47:48.540 --> 00:47:51.970
are really at understanding
that particular family

00:47:51.970 --> 00:47:53.870
and its unique challenges and strengths,

00:47:53.870 --> 00:47:55.760
and then telling that family narrative

00:47:55.760 --> 00:47:57.380
from the perspective of the caregivers,

00:47:57.380 --> 00:47:59.829
but also then from the
perspective of the children,

00:47:59.829 --> 00:48:02.207
and then moving through
the different skills

00:48:02.207 --> 00:48:05.190
and bringing it all together
with a family meeting,

00:48:05.190 --> 00:48:06.023
which is also

00:48:06.023 --> 00:48:08.340
a very critical component
of Beard intervention.

00:48:08.340 --> 00:48:09.380
And these family meetings

00:48:09.380 --> 00:48:11.470
are something that
families may go on to do

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:13.963
after the brief preventative intervention.

00:48:13.963 --> 00:48:17.821
So we're gonna share with
you some initial results

00:48:17.821 --> 00:48:21.347
from the NIMHD funded R24,

00:48:22.480 --> 00:48:23.670
which was a pilot grant,

00:48:23.670 --> 00:48:25.570
to really look at
feasibility, acceptability,

00:48:25.570 --> 00:48:28.380
it's underpowered to get
huge statistical results.

00:48:28.380 --> 00:48:29.770
We're in that trial now.

00:48:29.770 --> 00:48:31.920
But the intention was
to enroll 80 families,

00:48:31.920 --> 00:48:34.832
40 from each community, have
pre and post-assessment,

00:48:34.832 --> 00:48:36.903
and have a randomization.

00:48:37.970 --> 00:48:40.740
There are good usual care
services in these communities.

00:48:40.740 --> 00:48:42.090
And so it just like a lottery,

00:48:42.090 --> 00:48:43.840
some families, in addition to usual care

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:45.128
would get the home visitor.

00:48:45.128 --> 00:48:47.560
And then all along the
way you heard from Bhuwan

00:48:47.560 --> 00:48:49.330
about the importance of our
community advisory board.

00:48:49.330 --> 00:48:52.490
So whenever we had hit
snags or needed input,

00:48:52.490 --> 00:48:54.090
especially in the
intervention development,

00:48:54.090 --> 00:48:57.160
but also as we went, giving
updates and getting feedback

00:48:57.160 --> 00:49:01.160
from the community advisory
boards in a CBPR manner,

00:49:01.160 --> 00:49:04.820
and documenting that in a
process evaluation as we go.

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:06.030
And this is all guided

00:49:06.030 --> 00:49:09.410
by an implementation science
framework that we call EPIS,

00:49:09.410 --> 00:49:12.480
that really looks at these
different phases of exploration,

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:14.390
preparation after the intervention,

00:49:14.390 --> 00:49:16.560
actual studying of the implementation,

00:49:16.560 --> 00:49:19.370
and then questions towards sustainment,

00:49:19.370 --> 00:49:21.430
and thinking about these bridging factors

00:49:21.430 --> 00:49:25.410
between the community,
between the actual features,

00:49:25.410 --> 00:49:26.780
the inner context of the intervention,

00:49:26.780 --> 00:49:29.240
how hard it is to do,
the materials we use,

00:49:29.240 --> 00:49:31.660
making it more easy to use
than the outer context,

00:49:31.660 --> 00:49:33.350
the funding, the policy context,

00:49:33.350 --> 00:49:35.260
and all of that's ever changing.

00:49:35.260 --> 00:49:38.090
So in the data I'll show you today,

00:49:38.090 --> 00:49:41.020
the main research questions
were understanding, one...

00:49:41.020 --> 00:49:42.210
I'm gonna show you the qual data,

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:44.010
and then a little bit of the quant data.

00:49:44.010 --> 00:49:46.840
And we also are working on
a mixed methods paper now,

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:48.382
where we synthesize those.

00:49:48.382 --> 00:49:51.280
But the questions were how
acceptable and feasible

00:49:51.280 --> 00:49:52.990
is the family strengthening

00:49:52.990 --> 00:49:55.523
intervention for refugee
families or FSI-R?

00:49:55.523 --> 00:49:57.080
And what contributes to that?

00:49:57.080 --> 00:49:58.140
What kind of changes if any,

00:49:58.140 --> 00:49:59.800
do caregivers and children

00:49:59.800 --> 00:50:01.758
observe in themselves and their families

00:50:01.758 --> 00:50:03.930
from the intervention participation,

00:50:03.930 --> 00:50:05.490
and what perspectives do they have

00:50:05.490 --> 00:50:07.093
for improving this intervention?

00:50:07.940 --> 00:50:10.920
And so I'll show you data that
comes from 36 exit interviews

00:50:10.920 --> 00:50:12.826
with caregivers and with children,

00:50:12.826 --> 00:50:14.640
that looked at those research questions.

00:50:14.640 --> 00:50:15.730
These were double coded

00:50:15.730 --> 00:50:18.580
and analyzed using both
grounded theory techniques,

00:50:18.580 --> 00:50:20.260
but also thematic content analysis,

00:50:20.260 --> 00:50:22.430
because we have some theory-driven codes.

00:50:22.430 --> 00:50:23.560
And so our sample

00:50:23.560 --> 00:50:26.340
is half and half Somali
Bantu, and Bhutanese,

00:50:26.340 --> 00:50:30.310
10 families from the Somali Bantu side,

00:50:30.310 --> 00:50:31.993
11 Bhutanese families.

00:50:31.993 --> 00:50:33.393
And the main themes

00:50:33.393 --> 00:50:34.790
when it comes to acceptability

00:50:34.790 --> 00:50:36.294
and feasibility of
doing this intervention,

00:50:36.294 --> 00:50:37.570
we're really about,

00:50:37.570 --> 00:50:38.403
as you might imagine,

00:50:38.403 --> 00:50:40.360
just scheduling as a
barrier and overcoming that,

00:50:40.360 --> 00:50:42.290
but also experiences
with discussed the past,

00:50:42.290 --> 00:50:44.180
and I mentioned that in
the conceptual model.

00:50:44.180 --> 00:50:46.450
And the relationship with
the interventionists.

00:50:46.450 --> 00:50:47.960
And so as you might imagine,

00:50:47.960 --> 00:50:49.960
these are families that
are working many jobs,

00:50:49.960 --> 00:50:51.370
oftentimes they're large families,

00:50:51.370 --> 00:50:53.579
scheduling issues could be a barrier,

00:50:53.579 --> 00:50:56.551
but home visiting really
helps us get far with that.

00:50:56.551 --> 00:50:59.800
There was once a center-based
attempt at this intervention

00:50:59.800 --> 00:51:03.420
that really failed because
of low participation rates.

00:51:03.420 --> 00:51:04.891
And the participants share

00:51:04.891 --> 00:51:07.140
that that intervention
has problem-solving them

00:51:07.140 --> 00:51:10.550
with such an important
part of making it feasible.

00:51:10.550 --> 00:51:11.587
So as this mother explains,

00:51:11.587 --> 00:51:12.662
"They would call me beforehand,

00:51:12.662 --> 00:51:13.989
make sure I was available

00:51:13.989 --> 00:51:16.260
and I'd tell them to
come over if I was home,

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:19.347
and we have to do a lot of
double and triple checking."

00:51:19.347 --> 00:51:21.390
The Somali Bantu mother

00:51:21.390 --> 00:51:23.380
talks about the patience
of the interventionist,

00:51:23.380 --> 00:51:26.110
how sometimes they'd argue
and he would tolerate them

00:51:26.110 --> 00:51:27.520
and also was very flexible

00:51:27.520 --> 00:51:29.380
about having to reschedule.

00:51:29.380 --> 00:51:32.230
The Bhutanese father here
found it was easy and flexible.

00:51:33.110 --> 00:51:35.600
Experiences discussing the past.

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:39.770
As I mentioned, as a
trauma-informed intervention,

00:51:39.770 --> 00:51:42.620
this family narrative meant
that this was an opportunity

00:51:42.620 --> 00:51:44.963
for individuals to learn
about the family history

00:51:44.963 --> 00:51:48.280
and even for children for the first time

00:51:48.280 --> 00:51:49.410
to hear about the history of their family

00:51:49.410 --> 00:51:50.470
and how they come to United States.

00:51:50.470 --> 00:51:52.220
But sometimes, for parents,

00:51:52.220 --> 00:51:53.940
who'd been through difficult
trauma in the past,

00:51:53.940 --> 00:51:56.510
just going back to any memory of the past

00:51:56.510 --> 00:51:58.640
could be triggering or difficult.

00:51:58.640 --> 00:51:59.473
And so we have to do

00:51:59.473 --> 00:52:02.420
a lot of training with
our interventionists,

00:52:02.420 --> 00:52:03.950
focusing on the strengths,

00:52:03.950 --> 00:52:05.730
focusing on the journey forward,

00:52:05.730 --> 00:52:07.220
and that they're here
today and they're safe,

00:52:07.220 --> 00:52:09.600
and what's the future that
they look forward together.

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:11.447
And so here's this Somali girl saying,

00:52:11.447 --> 00:52:12.280
"My favorite part

00:52:12.280 --> 00:52:14.180
was when we talked about
my grandparents and stuff,

00:52:14.180 --> 00:52:15.770
my mom never talked about them before,

00:52:15.770 --> 00:52:18.280
and I like knowing about them."

00:52:18.280 --> 00:52:19.286
Or the Somali mother,

00:52:19.286 --> 00:52:21.170
"Most of the intervention was fine,

00:52:21.170 --> 00:52:23.020
but the only thing I'd say
I didn't feel comfortable

00:52:23.020 --> 00:52:24.640
is talking about what happened in the past

00:52:24.640 --> 00:52:26.370
in Somalia and in the refugee camp,

00:52:26.370 --> 00:52:28.920
life was it easy for me and my family."

00:52:28.920 --> 00:52:30.287
Here's a Bhutanese boy who said,

00:52:30.287 --> 00:52:32.250
"The interventionists
asked us about family,

00:52:32.250 --> 00:52:33.320
what happened in the past,

00:52:33.320 --> 00:52:34.960
all of that, I felt good about.

00:52:34.960 --> 00:52:37.170
He asked about my birthplace
and everything after that,

00:52:37.170 --> 00:52:39.060
I had forgotten a lot of things.

00:52:39.060 --> 00:52:41.757
I got to know things I
didn't know because of him."

00:52:43.340 --> 00:52:44.690
And so when it comes to

00:52:44.690 --> 00:52:46.147
the relationship with the interventionist,

00:52:46.147 --> 00:52:47.850
participants spoke very highly

00:52:47.850 --> 00:52:49.220
of having someone from the community

00:52:49.220 --> 00:52:52.410
describing them as respectful,
patient, understanding,

00:52:52.410 --> 00:52:54.648
and seeing them as a
real source of knowledge.

00:52:54.648 --> 00:52:57.160
And so the fact that they
were part of the community,

00:52:57.160 --> 00:53:00.240
really contributed to the
acceptability of the intervention.

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:01.567
So here's a Somali Bantu mother,

00:53:01.567 --> 00:53:03.320
"I think the interventionist
did a good job.

00:53:03.320 --> 00:53:05.210
I can't see how anyone
else in the community

00:53:05.210 --> 00:53:06.270
who'd be able to do that.

00:53:06.270 --> 00:53:07.103
It was very helpful

00:53:07.103 --> 00:53:09.160
to see someone from the
community work in the project,

00:53:09.160 --> 00:53:11.170
and we understand each other."

00:53:11.170 --> 00:53:13.207
Here's a Somali Bantu mother also,

00:53:13.207 --> 00:53:15.470
"The interventionist is
to someone who understands

00:53:15.470 --> 00:53:17.270
the dynamic and the
function of the intervention

00:53:17.270 --> 00:53:19.337
and the Somali Bantu culture."

00:53:20.370 --> 00:53:22.950
So when talking about the
impact on participants,

00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:24.354
primary themes that emerge

00:53:24.354 --> 00:53:26.967
relate to improving communication.

00:53:26.967 --> 00:53:29.690
More positive time spent
together as a family

00:53:29.690 --> 00:53:32.250
and also the relationship
between caregivers

00:53:32.250 --> 00:53:34.830
as well as relationship between
children and caregivers.

00:53:34.830 --> 00:53:38.770
And so for family
communication, we see that,

00:53:38.770 --> 00:53:40.070
especially for Bhutanese families,

00:53:40.070 --> 00:53:41.470
they talked about how the intervention

00:53:41.470 --> 00:53:43.610
led children to share
more with their parents

00:53:43.610 --> 00:53:44.950
and vice versa.

00:53:44.950 --> 00:53:46.150
Parents took more initiative

00:53:46.150 --> 00:53:48.520
to talk with their
children about their lives.

00:53:48.520 --> 00:53:50.580
And for many Somali Bantu participants,

00:53:50.580 --> 00:53:53.790
they talked about the
increased time together,

00:53:53.790 --> 00:53:56.850
discussion time as a family,
but language barriers,

00:53:56.850 --> 00:53:59.760
they'll continue to hinder
family communication.

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:00.887
So here's a Bhutanese mother,

00:54:00.887 --> 00:54:02.900
"I learned that we should
not avoid children.

00:54:02.900 --> 00:54:04.430
We used to do things in certain ways

00:54:04.430 --> 00:54:05.680
because we didn't understand.

00:54:05.680 --> 00:54:07.810
But now we understand that
we should talk to each other

00:54:07.810 --> 00:54:09.810
and ask about how the
other person is doing,

00:54:09.810 --> 00:54:11.710
who he's with, where he's going.

00:54:11.710 --> 00:54:12.543
When I said

00:54:12.543 --> 00:54:14.490
he could talk about these
things with his mother,

00:54:14.490 --> 00:54:16.260
we still share things
between mother and son.

00:54:16.260 --> 00:54:19.100
Now we know things happening
in each other's lives."

00:54:19.100 --> 00:54:20.337
Or here's a Somali Bantu girl,

00:54:20.337 --> 00:54:22.640
"Just to talk with my family was the best.

00:54:22.640 --> 00:54:24.360
I never talk with my parents like that."

00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:25.550
And sometimes culturally,

00:54:25.550 --> 00:54:27.689
and especially, we see
this in Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:54:27.689 --> 00:54:30.640
it's not typical that
you would speak directly

00:54:30.640 --> 00:54:32.300
to your parents about your life,

00:54:32.300 --> 00:54:33.133
and if you had problems,

00:54:33.133 --> 00:54:34.850
you might go through an intermediary.

00:54:34.850 --> 00:54:37.260
And we still respect a
lot of those approaches

00:54:37.260 --> 00:54:38.930
when we help families problem-solve

00:54:38.930 --> 00:54:40.261
when they're facing challenges.

00:54:40.261 --> 00:54:43.520
But also just opening up
communication channels

00:54:43.520 --> 00:54:45.590
has been a huge part of the intervention.

00:54:45.590 --> 00:54:46.610
Spending time together,

00:54:46.610 --> 00:54:49.090
they talked about enjoying time together.

00:54:49.090 --> 00:54:50.237
This Bhutanese mother,

00:54:50.237 --> 00:54:52.210
"I didn't know how to give
time to children at home.

00:54:52.210 --> 00:54:53.818
I liked that, we learned a lot.

00:54:53.818 --> 00:54:55.330
We got to give them time.

00:54:55.330 --> 00:54:57.820
I learnt this should have been
like this for generations."

00:54:57.820 --> 00:54:59.627
Or the Somali Bantu mother, another one,

00:54:59.627 --> 00:55:00.460
"The intervention

00:55:00.460 --> 00:55:02.140
affected family
relationships in a good way.

00:55:02.140 --> 00:55:04.720
None of my children used to talk before.

00:55:04.720 --> 00:55:06.870
Now we sometimes sit
and watch TV together.

00:55:06.870 --> 00:55:08.690
I always remind them
about the interventionists

00:55:08.690 --> 00:55:09.915
and what he told them."

00:55:09.915 --> 00:55:12.387
Here's a Bhutanese girl, a 12-year-old,

00:55:12.387 --> 00:55:14.650
"The thing that was really
helpful for our family

00:55:14.650 --> 00:55:16.420
is to go to different
places, to make plans."

00:55:16.420 --> 00:55:17.890
Now this is pre-COVID,

00:55:17.890 --> 00:55:19.660
But there are still ways
we're helping families

00:55:19.660 --> 00:55:21.380
think about making there

00:55:21.380 --> 00:55:25.320
be really pleasant time
together shared as a family.

00:55:25.320 --> 00:55:26.343
She talked about,

00:55:27.630 --> 00:55:31.170
talking about very simple
outings to a park, a theme park,

00:55:31.170 --> 00:55:33.163
a water park, they felt closer.

00:55:34.000 --> 00:55:34.897
And she said,

00:55:34.897 --> 00:55:37.480
"Before this, my mom and dad
had a really busy schedule.

00:55:37.480 --> 00:55:38.730
They made more time for us,

00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:41.640
so we had more time to
go out and be a family."

00:55:41.640 --> 00:55:43.390
Relationships between caregivers.

00:55:43.390 --> 00:55:46.050
So it's different between
these two communities,

00:55:46.050 --> 00:55:47.350
but again, it's a small sample.

00:55:47.350 --> 00:55:49.694
A lot of Somali Bantu
mentioned the intervention.

00:55:49.694 --> 00:55:51.720
Very few talked about it

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:53.570
really changing the
caregiver relationship.

00:55:53.570 --> 00:55:54.760
The Bhutanese stated

00:55:54.760 --> 00:55:56.990
how their relationships had been positive.

00:55:56.990 --> 00:55:57.823
but children really

00:55:57.823 --> 00:56:00.330
did observe changes
between the caregivers.

00:56:00.330 --> 00:56:01.247
Here's a Somali Bantu mother,

00:56:01.247 --> 00:56:02.080
"I love my husband.

00:56:02.080 --> 00:56:03.500
Was it supposed to change?"

00:56:03.500 --> 00:56:05.917
Here's a Bhutanese boy who
said, what he observed was,

00:56:05.917 --> 00:56:07.710
"Well, before my dad
just came back from work,

00:56:07.710 --> 00:56:09.190
slept, said nothing to my mother.

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:10.190
Now he comes back from work

00:56:10.190 --> 00:56:11.900
and actually sits down
and talks to my mother

00:56:11.900 --> 00:56:13.830
how her day was, they talk to each other.

00:56:13.830 --> 00:56:15.410
I think they found a better relationship

00:56:15.410 --> 00:56:16.800
when they took part in the intervention.

00:56:16.800 --> 00:56:17.880
I think they understand

00:56:17.880 --> 00:56:19.920
and they like each other more."

00:56:19.920 --> 00:56:22.300
So when it comes to
improving the intervention,

00:56:22.300 --> 00:56:24.960
the kind of feedback that they gave

00:56:24.960 --> 00:56:27.710
were one about seeking tangible skills,

00:56:27.710 --> 00:56:30.180
which you can imagine, illiteracy, jobs,

00:56:30.180 --> 00:56:31.740
these things still really matter.

00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:33.960
And also moving from a family focus,

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:34.793
also to think about,

00:56:34.793 --> 00:56:36.010
how can there be more

00:56:36.010 --> 00:56:38.923
leverage of the strengths
in the communities.

00:56:39.850 --> 00:56:42.170
So seeking tangible skills.

00:56:42.170 --> 00:56:43.087
Even though the intervention

00:56:43.087 --> 00:56:45.740
was doing really exciting
things on the family dynamics,

00:56:45.740 --> 00:56:47.980
they talked about so many
other services they need.

00:56:47.980 --> 00:56:49.797
And you see this from
the Somali Bantu mother,

00:56:49.797 --> 00:56:51.260
"I think you need to start services

00:56:51.260 --> 00:56:52.890
for the people like English classes,

00:56:52.890 --> 00:56:55.680
parenting classes, youth
group for both boys and girls,

00:56:55.680 --> 00:56:58.200
parent groups, money saving programs."

00:56:58.200 --> 00:57:00.400
Here's a Bhutanese father
talking about disability,

00:57:00.400 --> 00:57:04.720
which was pretty high within
the Bhutanese community,

00:57:04.720 --> 00:57:06.550
and remains to be a dynamic

00:57:06.550 --> 00:57:09.310
of the resettled populations
we're working with.

00:57:09.310 --> 00:57:14.113
And also alcohol abuse being
another important topic.

00:57:14.113 --> 00:57:16.284
Whether connecting them to the services

00:57:16.284 --> 00:57:18.900
or some financial assistance or education

00:57:18.900 --> 00:57:20.210
and transporting them,

00:57:20.210 --> 00:57:23.520
those are all things that
Bhutanese parents were feeling

00:57:23.520 --> 00:57:25.310
they still needed attention to.

00:57:25.310 --> 00:57:26.143
Here's a Bhutanese boy,

00:57:26.143 --> 00:57:27.720
"My mom and dad can't speak English.

00:57:27.720 --> 00:57:28.820
If they need to go to the hospital,

00:57:28.820 --> 00:57:29.850
you need to be able to speak English.

00:57:29.850 --> 00:57:31.710
So to help our parents with their English,

00:57:31.710 --> 00:57:33.410
that would help us a lot."

00:57:33.410 --> 00:57:36.260
And then we saw this dynamic,

00:57:36.260 --> 00:57:37.960
especially coming from the youth,

00:57:37.960 --> 00:57:38.793
to talk about

00:57:38.793 --> 00:57:40.690
how can we also do things
at the community level?

00:57:40.690 --> 00:57:41.567
So here's the Bhutanese girl,

00:57:41.567 --> 00:57:43.520
"If there was a hall for
children to come together

00:57:43.520 --> 00:57:46.750
to play, dance, talk, learn
Nepali language and culture,

00:57:46.750 --> 00:57:48.230
we can't afford that for our kids."

00:57:48.230 --> 00:57:49.147
Or this young boy,

00:57:49.147 --> 00:57:51.040
"Services like youth
groups or women's groups

00:57:51.040 --> 00:57:52.850
or men's groups or couples groups

00:57:52.850 --> 00:57:53.820
to bring the community together

00:57:53.820 --> 00:57:55.960
at least once a month or
twice would be great."

00:57:55.960 --> 00:57:57.790
And that's why our community meetings

00:57:57.790 --> 00:58:00.280
are oftentimes an event with food.

00:58:00.280 --> 00:58:02.030
We've had cultural events together,

00:58:02.030 --> 00:58:04.589
and that's why working with
community leaders like Bhuwan

00:58:04.589 --> 00:58:07.070
is so important for hearing
this kind of feedback,

00:58:07.070 --> 00:58:09.636
and for them to think
about what they can do

00:58:09.636 --> 00:58:12.770
with those requests from the community.

00:58:12.770 --> 00:58:14.660
When it comes to the quantitative results,

00:58:14.660 --> 00:58:15.560
and these are just published

00:58:15.560 --> 00:58:18.089
in the Journal of Adolescent
Health if you're interested.

00:58:18.089 --> 00:58:20.270
With this pre to post-data collection,

00:58:20.270 --> 00:58:22.140
we did see in the children,

00:58:22.140 --> 00:58:25.300
a significant reduction in
those getting the intervention

00:58:25.300 --> 00:58:27.890
and their reports of
traumatic stress reactions.

00:58:27.890 --> 00:58:29.510
We also saw from caregiver reports,

00:58:29.510 --> 00:58:31.360
fewer child depression symptoms

00:58:31.360 --> 00:58:33.810
reported by caregivers
for both communities.

00:58:33.810 --> 00:58:35.500
And then conduct problems,
kind of both ways,

00:58:35.500 --> 00:58:36.333
for the Bhutanese,

00:58:36.333 --> 00:58:37.670
we saw a significant reduction

00:58:37.670 --> 00:58:39.500
in this small sample in conduct problems,

00:58:39.500 --> 00:58:40.605
but for the Somali Bantu,

00:58:40.605 --> 00:58:41.880
we actually saw that

00:58:41.880 --> 00:58:43.350
it looked higher in
the intervention group.

00:58:43.350 --> 00:58:44.360
So some of that

00:58:44.360 --> 00:58:46.664
could be due to variability
and small samples.

00:58:46.664 --> 00:58:49.100
But definitely the general trend

00:58:49.100 --> 00:58:54.100
is there are exciting shifts
in child mental health

00:58:54.480 --> 00:58:55.730
in the small sample.

00:58:55.730 --> 00:58:57.946
We also saw in the Bhutanese
side of the equation,

00:58:57.946 --> 00:59:01.030
a report in reductions in family arguing.

00:59:01.030 --> 00:59:01.863
And when it came

00:59:01.863 --> 00:59:03.990
to feasibility and acceptability
for both communities,

00:59:03.990 --> 00:59:07.060
extremely high reports of feasibility

00:59:07.060 --> 00:59:10.063
and also high reports of
satisfaction and acceptability.

00:59:11.020 --> 00:59:12.670
And so future steps.

00:59:12.670 --> 00:59:14.300
And I wanna make sure that

00:59:14.300 --> 00:59:16.150
we have a little time to talk about COVID.

00:59:16.150 --> 00:59:17.080
As you might imagine,

00:59:17.080 --> 00:59:19.770
and as Bhuwan mentioned
at our introduction,

00:59:19.770 --> 00:59:23.020
the policy and the political
landscape are ever shifting.

00:59:23.020 --> 00:59:26.070
If you can imagine what this
project has seen in the years,

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:29.060
it started from 2004, 2005 to 2020,

00:59:29.060 --> 00:59:33.910
a lot of shifts in policy
funding, resettlement dynamics.

00:59:33.910 --> 00:59:35.610
When we originally developed this

00:59:35.610 --> 00:59:38.170
being done by community lay workers

00:59:38.170 --> 00:59:40.150
or community health worker provisions

00:59:40.150 --> 00:59:41.380
within the Affordable Care Act

00:59:41.380 --> 00:59:43.610
and also provisions for
prevention services,

00:59:43.610 --> 00:59:45.170
were really a big part of our vision

00:59:45.170 --> 00:59:49.610
for how we might have this
scalable and reimbursable,

00:59:49.610 --> 00:59:51.290
should it be evidence-based,

00:59:51.290 --> 00:59:54.560
should it continue to
show an evidence-based.

00:59:54.560 --> 00:59:56.120
And that's meant we had to really focus

00:59:56.120 --> 00:59:58.880
on state level policies
in Massachusetts and Maine

00:59:58.880 --> 01:00:01.640
around prevention services
and what's reimbursable

01:00:01.640 --> 01:00:04.250
and who the delivery agents
can be, including lay workers,

01:00:04.250 --> 01:00:06.530
and we need more on,

01:00:06.530 --> 01:00:07.780
is it truly effective?

01:00:07.780 --> 01:00:09.030
And that's why we're finishing now

01:00:09.030 --> 01:00:10.660
a well-powered effectiveness study,

01:00:10.660 --> 01:00:13.810
as well as the process evaluation
steps we've talked about.

01:00:13.810 --> 01:00:16.801
So Bhuwan, I'll turn it back
to you to talk about COVID-19.

01:00:16.801 --> 01:00:20.940
- [Orazio] Theresa, can I
ask a couple of questions,

01:00:20.940 --> 01:00:23.330
or should we wait to the end?

01:00:25.040 --> 01:00:26.270
- [Orazio] You can ask them now.

01:00:26.270 --> 01:00:27.943
Is that Orazio?

01:00:27.943 --> 01:00:29.835
- [Orazio] Yeah, it is me.

01:00:29.835 --> 01:00:30.668
- [Theresa] Great.

01:00:30.668 --> 01:00:33.683
- [Orazio] But I am happy
either way, if you wanna-

01:00:35.435 --> 01:00:36.268
- We're almost done.

01:00:36.268 --> 01:00:37.480
Let us finish these last slides

01:00:37.480 --> 01:00:39.228
and then we'll just open it up.

01:00:39.228 --> 01:00:41.030
- [Orazio] Okay.

01:00:41.030 --> 01:00:44.270
- Due to COVID, it's really challenging.

01:00:44.270 --> 01:00:47.720
Before COVID we used to
collect our data in-person,

01:00:47.720 --> 01:00:51.452
now with the COVID we are
collecting data remotely,

01:00:51.452 --> 01:00:56.452
and our bilingual, bicultural
Nepali speaking trend,

01:00:57.070 --> 01:00:59.800
family strengthening intervention staff,

01:00:59.800 --> 01:01:02.123
they are the ones who
do the data collection.

01:01:02.980 --> 01:01:04.738
Besides the intervention work

01:01:04.738 --> 01:01:06.892
at the request of the communities

01:01:06.892 --> 01:01:09.940
we have supported in providing

01:01:09.940 --> 01:01:12.290
vitally important public
health information,

01:01:12.290 --> 01:01:15.870
especially around
debunking some of the myths

01:01:15.870 --> 01:01:18.760
like eating ginger, garlic

01:01:18.760 --> 01:01:22.600
or steam Vicks would water off the virus.

01:01:22.600 --> 01:01:27.600
We were able to bring
epidemiologists and researchers

01:01:28.294 --> 01:01:33.294
and conducted those information
session and outreach,

01:01:34.850 --> 01:01:38.143
and provided education via Facebook Live

01:01:38.143 --> 01:01:42.843
with live interpretations
for our Bhutanese population.

01:01:43.720 --> 01:01:45.470
And our upcoming one

01:01:45.470 --> 01:01:47.794
is about supporting the
students and families

01:01:47.794 --> 01:01:51.270
in the Bhutanese community
with remote learning

01:01:51.270 --> 01:01:52.740
during this COVID-19.

01:01:52.740 --> 01:01:54.270
Parents are frustrated,

01:01:54.270 --> 01:01:56.120
children are everywhere, it's a mess,

01:01:57.620 --> 01:01:59.230
especially in our community

01:01:59.230 --> 01:02:01.330
where parents don't know what's happening.

01:02:02.820 --> 01:02:03.653
And with that,

01:02:03.653 --> 01:02:05.930
I would like to turn it
over to Dr. Betancourt,

01:02:05.930 --> 01:02:08.370
to talk about digital manual.

01:02:08.370 --> 01:02:09.610
- Sure, thanks, Bhuwan.

01:02:09.610 --> 01:02:12.520
And just on the Somali Bantu
side, it's interesting,

01:02:12.520 --> 01:02:14.450
just their user preferences
and differences.

01:02:14.450 --> 01:02:16.390
So Facebook Live makes
sense for the Bhutanese,

01:02:16.390 --> 01:02:19.500
but for the Somali Bantu,
they don't use Facebook Live.

01:02:19.500 --> 01:02:21.000
And so to reach those communities,

01:02:21.000 --> 01:02:23.230
we're working with the
local community leaders

01:02:23.230 --> 01:02:25.100
using WhatsApp groups.

01:02:25.100 --> 01:02:27.550
They do a lot of psycho-education
out of their center,

01:02:27.550 --> 01:02:30.510
where they're still in a socially
distant way wearing mask,

01:02:30.510 --> 01:02:34.140
holding some sessions on
education for families.

01:02:34.140 --> 01:02:36.830
So it's all about working
with the community

01:02:36.830 --> 01:02:39.540
and understanding their dynamic,

01:02:39.540 --> 01:02:40.810
their needs, their preferences.

01:02:40.810 --> 01:02:42.670
And related to this,

01:02:42.670 --> 01:02:43.890
as you can imagine,

01:02:43.890 --> 01:02:46.556
trying to do the intervention
solely over the phone,

01:02:46.556 --> 01:02:50.500
now that it's impossible to do
face-to-face data collection

01:02:50.500 --> 01:02:52.360
has been really challenging.

01:02:52.360 --> 01:02:53.510
What's really exciting

01:02:53.510 --> 01:02:56.421
is we had prior to COVID
launched this partnership

01:02:56.421 --> 01:02:59.860
with Boston College
Computer Science Department.

01:02:59.860 --> 01:03:01.610
And Sunand Bhattacharya,

01:03:01.610 --> 01:03:03.830
the Vice Provost for Design and Innovation

01:03:03.830 --> 01:03:06.307
with a small internal Boston College grant

01:03:06.307 --> 01:03:09.156
to develop a technology platform

01:03:09.156 --> 01:03:14.156
where we could swap in the
language, the graphics,

01:03:14.406 --> 01:03:17.877
to quickly adapt the manual
for different refugee groups.

01:03:17.877 --> 01:03:20.550
And this was a part of
our scaling strategy.

01:03:20.550 --> 01:03:22.720
And so with COVID-19,

01:03:22.720 --> 01:03:26.150
new energy really came into
that process of thinking,

01:03:26.150 --> 01:03:28.570
how can we use digital supports

01:03:28.570 --> 01:03:31.280
for the community health
worker to do the sessions

01:03:31.280 --> 01:03:32.850
in a way that's engaging.

01:03:32.850 --> 01:03:33.683
And now we've really

01:03:33.683 --> 01:03:36.920
started to focus on the
family-facing version of the app,

01:03:36.920 --> 01:03:39.390
which would be done with a family together

01:03:39.390 --> 01:03:40.870
using a tablet at their household,

01:03:40.870 --> 01:03:42.440
rather than people on Zoom,

01:03:42.440 --> 01:03:43.550
which we're still able to do

01:03:43.550 --> 01:03:45.580
sometimes by phone, sometimes by Zoom.

01:03:45.580 --> 01:03:46.810
But to make it more engaging

01:03:46.810 --> 01:03:49.299
because they're getting
interrupted, there's lots of kids,

01:03:49.299 --> 01:03:52.083
to use video, to use imagery

01:03:52.083 --> 01:03:54.750
has been a really important
part of this next phase,

01:03:54.750 --> 01:03:57.963
using human-centered design
and co-design techniques.

01:03:59.821 --> 01:04:03.404
And the idea is if we
create using a platform

01:04:03.404 --> 01:04:06.070
that could work on a
smartphone or a tablet,

01:04:06.070 --> 01:04:07.719
now we have a flexible platform.

01:04:07.719 --> 01:04:09.220
As you think about

01:04:09.220 --> 01:04:13.580
the ever-changing migration
and refugee patterns

01:04:13.580 --> 01:04:15.420
in higher income countries

01:04:15.420 --> 01:04:17.630
where we can adapt these
interventions quickly

01:04:17.630 --> 01:04:18.463
with new imagery,

01:04:18.463 --> 01:04:20.472
with community-based processes,

01:04:20.472 --> 01:04:21.877
to think about what's appropriate

01:04:21.877 --> 01:04:23.240
in the intervention content

01:04:23.240 --> 01:04:24.830
and have different versions of it

01:04:24.830 --> 01:04:28.050
done by lay workers, that
could be readily scalable.

01:04:28.050 --> 01:04:30.330
And so that use of technology
has been really exciting.

01:04:30.330 --> 01:04:32.361
And right now we've got
a Somali Bantu version

01:04:32.361 --> 01:04:34.080
and a Bhutanese version,

01:04:34.080 --> 01:04:35.330
but you could see in the future

01:04:35.330 --> 01:04:38.860
we could have Afghani,
Congolese, Venezuelan.

01:04:38.860 --> 01:04:39.693
So that's a really

01:04:39.693 --> 01:04:42.210
exciting new development using technology.

01:04:42.210 --> 01:04:44.788
So just to wrap up so I can
open it up to questions.

01:04:44.788 --> 01:04:47.090
I hope we've been able to convince you

01:04:47.090 --> 01:04:48.690
that promoting mental health

01:04:48.690 --> 01:04:51.240
is about also looking to
the health of the family,

01:04:51.240 --> 01:04:53.578
and it's critical to longer term success

01:04:53.578 --> 01:04:57.503
of New Americans and investing
in their human capital.

01:04:58.390 --> 01:05:02.021
That to do this is really
going to take partnerships

01:05:02.021 --> 01:05:03.640
and changing the narrative

01:05:03.640 --> 01:05:05.150
to more of a strengths-based approach

01:05:05.150 --> 01:05:07.320
about New Americans and refugees.

01:05:07.320 --> 01:05:09.250
And community-based participatory research

01:05:09.250 --> 01:05:11.790
is a promising approach
for engaging communities,

01:05:11.790 --> 01:05:12.860
it's iterative,

01:05:12.860 --> 01:05:14.840
there's continuous integration
of lessons learned,

01:05:14.840 --> 01:05:17.750
but it also helps us to attend
to the concerns of community

01:05:17.750 --> 01:05:21.192
as well as do very fruitful,
interesting research together,

01:05:21.192 --> 01:05:24.150
and ultimately to arrive at interventions

01:05:24.150 --> 01:05:27.300
that are acceptable,
sustainable, feasible,

01:05:27.300 --> 01:05:29.433
and hopefully able to be scaled.

01:05:30.440 --> 01:05:31.273
So with that,

01:05:31.273 --> 01:05:33.630
I'll wrap up our formal presentation

01:05:33.630 --> 01:05:35.150
and we can open it up to questions.

01:05:35.150 --> 01:05:38.540
I did wanna mention that we are hiring.

01:05:38.540 --> 01:05:40.560
So those of you interested
in this kind of global work,

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:45.560
both in the United States as
well in Sub-Saharan Africa,

01:05:45.660 --> 01:05:48.820
here's the context, please
be in touch with us.

01:05:48.820 --> 01:05:50.870
We're doing this kind of work
in so many different settings,

01:05:50.870 --> 01:05:52.840
and we're just really
proud of this research

01:05:52.840 --> 01:05:55.000
with our Bhutanese and
Somali Bantu partners.

01:05:55.000 --> 01:05:57.240
Lot of references to our presentation

01:05:57.240 --> 01:05:58.700
if you're interested as well.

01:05:58.700 --> 01:06:01.430
So there you have it.

01:06:01.430 --> 01:06:02.530
Now we can open it up.

01:06:06.421 --> 01:06:07.790
- This is super interesting,

01:06:07.790 --> 01:06:10.667
thanks very much, Theresa and Bhuwan.

01:06:11.600 --> 01:06:14.520
The questions I have is
that I would be very curious

01:06:14.520 --> 01:06:16.460
to see the quantitative results,

01:06:16.460 --> 01:06:18.060
especially of the new study

01:06:20.203 --> 01:06:24.400
to quantify in a rigorous fashion,

01:06:24.400 --> 01:06:27.453
the impact on certain outcomes.

01:06:29.540 --> 01:06:30.970
I'm convinced that

01:06:32.250 --> 01:06:35.860
the emphasis on both the
family and the community,

01:06:35.860 --> 01:06:38.030
I was very impressed by
what you've been saying

01:06:38.030 --> 01:06:40.000
about the community meetings

01:06:40.000 --> 01:06:44.883
and trying to involve in some
fashion, the whole community.

01:06:46.200 --> 01:06:48.223
I find that very interesting.

01:06:49.230 --> 01:06:54.220
And I would like to see
if possible at some point,

01:06:54.220 --> 01:06:58.300
both the impacts on those mechanisms.

01:06:58.300 --> 01:06:59.300
So what is the impact

01:06:59.300 --> 01:07:02.790
of your intervention on
the community itself,

01:07:02.790 --> 01:07:04.790
on how much people trust each other,

01:07:04.790 --> 01:07:06.580
how much they collaborate.

01:07:06.580 --> 01:07:09.160
And there are a number
of quantitative measures

01:07:09.160 --> 01:07:14.160
that one can implement
and collect to do that.

01:07:15.340 --> 01:07:19.070
In addition to that, I would
also like to understand

01:07:21.861 --> 01:07:24.760
the extent to which
these community effects,

01:07:24.760 --> 01:07:28.300
and these family effects act as mediators

01:07:30.761 --> 01:07:31.594
from the final mental outcome.

01:07:34.420 --> 01:07:36.623
And finally, in terms of implementation,

01:07:37.920 --> 01:07:38.780
would love to see...

01:07:38.780 --> 01:07:40.910
I don't know whether there is power enough

01:07:40.910 --> 01:07:43.560
from the experiments that you're running.

01:07:43.560 --> 01:07:47.810
But something that would be
very interesting would be to,

01:07:47.810 --> 01:07:49.240
especially with COVID,

01:07:49.240 --> 01:07:52.870
especially with thinking
of how to run interventions

01:07:52.870 --> 01:07:54.280
with limited amount of resources,

01:07:54.280 --> 01:07:57.179
it would be really interesting to think of

01:07:57.179 --> 01:08:00.063
what aspect of the
interventions are crucial,

01:08:01.250 --> 01:08:05.980
are key to obtain certain
effects, and therefore...

01:08:05.980 --> 01:08:07.040
But in order to do that,

01:08:07.040 --> 01:08:08.450
you would have to have

01:08:08.450 --> 01:08:12.940
variability within the treatment
groups in those aspects.

01:08:12.940 --> 01:08:17.790
And sometimes, you can make
a virtue of some problems.

01:08:17.790 --> 01:08:20.850
So supposedly have some locations

01:08:20.850 --> 01:08:24.850
where the people running are
of higher quality, whatever,

01:08:24.850 --> 01:08:26.990
or you can randomly variate

01:08:26.990 --> 01:08:28.500
some aspects of the interventions.

01:08:28.500 --> 01:08:30.043
And I'll stop here.

01:08:31.030 --> 01:08:32.610
- Yeah, no,

01:08:32.610 --> 01:08:35.107
these are all really important
methodological issues,

01:08:35.107 --> 01:08:38.820
and it's exactly why Bhuwan is
now applying to PhD programs

01:08:38.820 --> 01:08:43.010
to help contribute to all of the richness

01:08:43.010 --> 01:08:44.410
that is still to be done.

01:08:44.410 --> 01:08:47.310
Just to give some brief
answers on the methods,

01:08:47.310 --> 01:08:48.650
and then Bhuwan should talk about

01:08:48.650 --> 01:08:50.300
the lived experience of the community,

01:08:50.300 --> 01:08:52.510
because he's certainly
more expert than I am.

01:08:52.510 --> 01:08:56.640
But I absolutely agree with
you that there are designs

01:08:56.640 --> 01:08:59.010
and measures that we can
do at the community level.

01:08:59.010 --> 01:09:00.800
In my public health training

01:09:00.800 --> 01:09:01.950
at the Harvard School of Public Health,

01:09:01.950 --> 01:09:04.150
I worked with Tony Earls on his project

01:09:04.150 --> 01:09:05.910
on Human Development in
Chicago Neighborhoods,

01:09:05.910 --> 01:09:08.810
which is famous for its paper and science

01:09:08.810 --> 01:09:11.520
on the measurement of collective efficacy,

01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:12.710
which is a synthesis

01:09:12.710 --> 01:09:15.420
of informal social control
and social cohesion.

01:09:15.420 --> 01:09:18.470
And they had an independent
neighborhood assessment of that.

01:09:18.470 --> 01:09:19.810
So it wasn't just self-reported

01:09:19.810 --> 01:09:21.000
by people in the intervention,

01:09:21.000 --> 01:09:22.040
but you could go door-to-door

01:09:22.040 --> 01:09:24.850
and get a measure of it by neighborhood.

01:09:24.850 --> 01:09:26.077
And wouldn't that be amazing,

01:09:26.077 --> 01:09:27.680
should be able to scale this

01:09:27.680 --> 01:09:30.130
to collect that kind of community data

01:09:30.130 --> 01:09:31.550
with these kinds of processes.

01:09:31.550 --> 01:09:33.870
Some communities randomly being allocated

01:09:33.870 --> 01:09:35.960
to be in this kind of CBPR work

01:09:35.960 --> 01:09:39.540
and others to be usual care control sites.

01:09:39.540 --> 01:09:41.050
That would be fascinating.

01:09:41.050 --> 01:09:43.580
And then when it comes
to the community effects

01:09:43.580 --> 01:09:46.790
and family effects acting
as mediators, absolutely.

01:09:46.790 --> 01:09:48.911
And that's sort of in
our conceptual model,

01:09:48.911 --> 01:09:52.960
that we really see if we can
shift the family dynamics.

01:09:52.960 --> 01:09:53.810
And that also means

01:09:53.810 --> 01:09:57.140
their navigation of community
relationships and resources,

01:09:57.140 --> 01:09:59.660
we believe by theory

01:09:59.660 --> 01:10:01.693
that we can improve the
mental health of the kids

01:10:01.693 --> 01:10:04.071
and see better family functioning.

01:10:04.071 --> 01:10:06.500
In 80-family pilot,

01:10:06.500 --> 01:10:07.480
there's not much we can do,

01:10:07.480 --> 01:10:11.630
in the 150-family data,
once we get that complete,

01:10:11.630 --> 01:10:13.120
we're about halfway there,

01:10:13.120 --> 01:10:15.211
maybe getting beyond halfway there now,

01:10:15.211 --> 01:10:16.895
but COVID has slowed us down,

01:10:16.895 --> 01:10:19.020
we'll have a little more statistical power

01:10:19.020 --> 01:10:22.270
to do those sorts of mediational analysis.

01:10:22.270 --> 01:10:24.010
And then lastly,

01:10:24.010 --> 01:10:26.970
when it comes to thinking
about dismantling studies

01:10:26.970 --> 01:10:28.700
or isolating the active ingredients,

01:10:28.700 --> 01:10:31.010
this is certainly a
topic of great interest

01:10:31.010 --> 01:10:33.540
in evidence-based behavioral
interventions of many sorts,

01:10:33.540 --> 01:10:35.640
mental health, early
childhood development.

01:10:35.640 --> 01:10:39.740
How do we make sure we're
doing the most powerful things

01:10:39.740 --> 01:10:41.650
and like removing the noise,

01:10:41.650 --> 01:10:42.970
and then also thinking about

01:10:42.970 --> 01:10:45.190
what works for whom
under what circumstances.

01:10:45.190 --> 01:10:47.409
So what modules given a family's makeup

01:10:47.409 --> 01:10:50.180
really need to happen with this family?

01:10:50.180 --> 01:10:51.960
And we do a lot of this.

01:10:51.960 --> 01:10:54.779
We have supplemental
modules that we may swap in

01:10:54.779 --> 01:10:56.309
and additional referrals

01:10:56.309 --> 01:10:59.620
that we often are making along
the process with the family,

01:10:59.620 --> 01:11:02.580
and substance abuse being a common theme

01:11:02.580 --> 01:11:04.190
that we've had to do a lot of referrals

01:11:04.190 --> 01:11:06.640
or treatment for
post-traumatic stress disorder,

01:11:06.640 --> 01:11:10.210
depression in the caregivers
or housing insecurity,

01:11:10.210 --> 01:11:12.472
food insecurity, legal issues.

01:11:12.472 --> 01:11:14.320
And so it would be very interesting

01:11:14.320 --> 01:11:16.360
to iterate those ingredients

01:11:16.360 --> 01:11:18.780
and use dismantling studies.

01:11:18.780 --> 01:11:20.100
On implementation science,

01:11:20.100 --> 01:11:21.820
we do have very interesting data

01:11:21.820 --> 01:11:24.870
that you may be interested
in, Orazio, on fidelity.

01:11:24.870 --> 01:11:28.190
And so our implementation science strategy

01:11:28.190 --> 01:11:29.190
that we're testing

01:11:30.030 --> 01:11:33.040
builds off of some of the work
we were doing in Sierra Leone,

01:11:33.040 --> 01:11:35.550
actually using collaborative teams,

01:11:35.550 --> 01:11:37.560
inter-agency collaborative teams

01:11:37.560 --> 01:11:39.690
to enhance the fidelity

01:11:39.690 --> 01:11:42.880
of the intervention done
by the home visitors.

01:11:42.880 --> 01:11:45.254
Now we have a small, because
we have two communities,

01:11:45.254 --> 01:11:46.792
our seed team of experts

01:11:46.792 --> 01:11:49.567
are people who've done
the intervention before,

01:11:49.567 --> 01:11:51.760
and they know it very well.

01:11:51.760 --> 01:11:53.290
They've been through it with many families

01:11:53.290 --> 01:11:56.037
and they then serve as an expert guide,

01:11:56.037 --> 01:11:57.420
knowing the culture

01:11:57.420 --> 01:12:00.030
and the language to
listen with permission.

01:12:00.030 --> 01:12:01.970
We get permission to
audio tape their sessions,

01:12:01.970 --> 01:12:02.960
and we have the home visitor

01:12:02.960 --> 01:12:04.897
ask permission to the family saying,

01:12:04.897 --> 01:12:06.410
"I'm learning to do this.

01:12:06.410 --> 01:12:08.160
Do I have your permission to audio tape?

01:12:08.160 --> 01:12:10.050
So that the expert and the intervention

01:12:10.050 --> 01:12:11.410
can hear how I'm doing

01:12:11.410 --> 01:12:14.150
and give me feedback so I can
do my best practice with you."

01:12:14.150 --> 01:12:18.403
And oftentimes, families
are very open to that.

01:12:18.403 --> 01:12:20.530
The recorder becomes a non-issue

01:12:20.530 --> 01:12:23.000
when you're really focused
on the work with the family,

01:12:23.000 --> 01:12:24.630
and then they're doing
targeted supervision.

01:12:24.630 --> 01:12:26.107
So they may come back and say,

01:12:26.107 --> 01:12:28.430
"Look, you're doing a
great job on this and that,

01:12:28.430 --> 01:12:30.380
but here's some areas
I wanna focus with you

01:12:30.380 --> 01:12:32.040
to prepare for your next session."

01:12:32.040 --> 01:12:35.080
And using that
collaborative team approach,

01:12:35.080 --> 01:12:36.640
we have team of experts,

01:12:36.640 --> 01:12:38.090
and we're really looking at fidelity,

01:12:38.090 --> 01:12:40.690
and we actually have
them score a checklist,

01:12:40.690 --> 01:12:42.030
a fidelity checklist,

01:12:42.030 --> 01:12:43.903
both about the session-specific elements

01:12:43.903 --> 01:12:48.370
and the cross-cutting just
performance on rapport,

01:12:48.370 --> 01:12:52.274
on relationship, on quality
that we have data on.

01:12:52.274 --> 01:12:53.770
So I'm watching the time.

01:12:53.770 --> 01:12:55.160
I know we don't have a
lot of time, Theresa,

01:12:55.160 --> 01:12:55.993
and I want Bhuwan

01:12:55.993 --> 01:12:58.403
to be able to speak about just
the impact on the community.

01:12:59.850 --> 01:13:01.773
- Sure, thank you for the question.

01:13:05.980 --> 01:13:08.250
That's what we have talked in our values

01:13:08.250 --> 01:13:10.193
about the CBPR approach.

01:13:12.419 --> 01:13:15.227
It takes a lot of preparation and time

01:13:15.227 --> 01:13:20.210
to invest in a community
which has low literacy rate.

01:13:20.210 --> 01:13:24.220
But again, I can give my own example that

01:13:25.330 --> 01:13:27.400
I had no prior idea about

01:13:27.400 --> 01:13:31.530
how to even to assess the
needs of our population,

01:13:31.530 --> 01:13:33.410
of our own community.

01:13:33.410 --> 01:13:35.006
Engaging people like me

01:13:35.006 --> 01:13:39.250
and additionally other non-skilled people

01:13:39.250 --> 01:13:41.260
to be community health workers.

01:13:41.260 --> 01:13:46.260
And then maybe that kind of resources,

01:13:46.740 --> 01:13:50.060
if we are able to
develop in the community.

01:13:50.060 --> 01:13:52.320
And also just to let you
know that our community

01:13:52.320 --> 01:13:54.970
is really a stratified society.

01:13:54.970 --> 01:13:57.580
We have lots of classes

01:13:57.580 --> 01:13:59.490
in all kinds of stratification,

01:13:59.490 --> 01:14:01.940
and makes easier to engage them

01:14:03.270 --> 01:14:06.614
with the different kinds
of background and those.

01:14:06.614 --> 01:14:11.017
So we'll be able to take their viewpoints.

01:14:11.017 --> 01:14:14.050
And also just to talk about

01:14:14.050 --> 01:14:15.950
developing building that trust,

01:14:15.950 --> 01:14:19.320
which is not easy thing to do
because refugee communities

01:14:19.320 --> 01:14:23.950
back in refugee camp have
been not treated fairly

01:14:23.950 --> 01:14:25.370
because of the politicians

01:14:25.370 --> 01:14:29.100
and all kinds of bad experiences
that they might have had,

01:14:29.100 --> 01:14:31.930
and unknown person coming
from the university

01:14:31.930 --> 01:14:34.230
and majority of them are white

01:14:34.230 --> 01:14:35.160
and they are coming

01:14:35.160 --> 01:14:36.860
and asking about all kinds of questions

01:14:36.860 --> 01:14:39.290
about their life background history,

01:14:39.290 --> 01:14:42.470
even the political public charges

01:14:42.470 --> 01:14:43.970
and all kinds of big things are coming.

01:14:43.970 --> 01:14:44.960
It's really scary.

01:14:44.960 --> 01:14:48.530
That is the reason we need to have real...

01:14:49.770 --> 01:14:51.099
We need to find out

01:14:51.099 --> 01:14:56.099
and reach out to the community
self-help organizations,

01:14:56.600 --> 01:14:57.580
faith-based organizations,

01:14:57.580 --> 01:15:00.690
to make sure that you work
with the community members

01:15:00.690 --> 01:15:03.410
who are well known in their community

01:15:03.410 --> 01:15:08.220
so that they can bring the
additional group of people.

01:15:08.220 --> 01:15:11.320
And then we'll able to
do the research work.

01:15:11.320 --> 01:15:13.600
Because it's really impossible
for researchers alone

01:15:13.600 --> 01:15:16.063
to build up the
strength-based interventions.

01:15:17.490 --> 01:15:21.050
And I think in terms of COVID,

01:15:21.050 --> 01:15:24.200
COVID has definitely brought challenges.

01:15:24.200 --> 01:15:27.600
And I think we really have an opportunity

01:15:27.600 --> 01:15:32.600
to train our community members,
